window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"brandIcon":"⛪","brandSubtitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062)","brandTitle":"Catholic Christianity (AQA RS A)","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / AQA A / Catholic Christianity","examBoard":"AQA GCSE","footerNote":"Original practice questions written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification, Component 1: Catholic Christianity, using the question format examined from June 2026 (1, 1, 4, 6 and 12 marks). Not past-paper material. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes; other valid answers are credited in the exam.","heroEyebrow":"Component 1 · Catholic Christianity · Beliefs \u0026 teachings · Practices","markingSchemaId":"aqa-rs-generic","pageTitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A: Catholic Christianity Revision","papers":[{"id":"Beliefs","label":"Beliefs \u0026 teachings","subtitle":"Key beliefs and the seven sacraments (1.1–1.7)","title":"Catholic Christianity: beliefs and teachings"},{"id":"Practices","label":"Practices","subtitle":"Worship and the work of the Church (2.1–2.8)","title":"Catholic Christianity: practices"}],"storageId":"aqa_rs_a_catholic_christianity","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Catholic Christianity"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Which one of the following councils first produced the Creed that declared the Son to be 'consubstantial' (of one being) with the Father?\nA) Council of Trent\nB) Council of Nicaea\nC) Second Vatican Council\nD) Council of Jerusalem","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Council of Nicaea.\n\nThe Council of Nicaea (AD 325) was called to answer Arius, who taught that the Son was a created being. It declared the Son to be 'of one substance' (homoousios) with the Father. Trent (1545–63) answered the Reformation. Vatican II (1962–65) renewed the Church in the modern world. The Council of Jerusalem (Acts 15) dealt with Gentile converts.","evidence":["Council of Nicaea, AD 325","homoousios / consubstantial","Arius"],"examTip":"In MCQs, cross out the options you know are wrong first. Dates help: Nicaea is the 4th century, Trent the 16th, Vatican II the 20th.","answerTopic":"Nicene Creed"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Which one of the following best describes the Catholic belief about God?\nA) Three gods who work together\nB) One God who appears in three different forms at different times\nC) One God in three distinct, co-equal persons\nD) One God the Father with two lesser helpers","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) One God in three distinct, co-equal persons.\n\nA is tritheism. B is modalism, the idea that God simply changes mode. D is close to Arianism, which ranks the Son and Spirit below the Father. The Church rejects all three. CCC 253 teaches 'one God in three persons'.","evidence":["CCC 253","Tritheism, modalism and Arianism are rejected"],"examTip":"Learn the three errors (tritheism, modalism, Arianism). Wrong MCQ options are often built from them.","answerTopic":"Trinity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Give one passage from the New Testament in which all three persons of the Trinity are present or named.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• The baptism of Jesus (Matthew 3:16–17 / Mark 1:9–11 / Luke 3:21–22)\n• The Great Commission, baptising 'in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit' (Matthew 28:19)\n• Paul's blessing in 2 Corinthians 13:13 (14)\n• Jesus promising the Father will send the Spirit in his name (John 14:26)","evidence":["Matthew 3:16–17","Matthew 28:19","2 Corinthians 13:13"],"examTip":"Only your first answer counts on 1-mark 'Give one' questions. Write one clear reference, not a list.","answerTopic":"Trinity in scripture"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Give one way in which Catholics show belief in the Trinity during worship.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Making the sign of the cross 'in the name of the Father, and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit'\n• Reciting the Nicene Creed at Sunday Mass\n• Praying the 'Glory be to the Father…' (doxology)\n• Baptising with water in the threefold name\n• The priest's Trinitarian blessing at the end of Mass","evidence":["Sign of the cross","Nicene Creed","Glory Be"],"examTip":"A simple, accurate practice is enough for 1 mark. You do not need to explain it.","answerTopic":"Trinity in worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Trinity influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics express the Trinity constantly in prayer and worship (1 mark). They begin and end prayers with the sign of the cross in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit. At Sunday Mass they profess the Nicene Creed, so the belief shapes their daily spiritual life and reminds them that God is with them in every activity (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Because God is a communion of three persons in a relationship of love, Catholics believe they too are made for relationship and community (1 mark). This encourages them to take part in parish life and to value family and friendship. They try to show self-giving love towards others, for example through charity work, because they reflect a God who is love (1 John 4:8) (2nd mark).","evidence":["1 John 4:8 'God is love'","Sign of the cross","Nicene Creed at Mass"],"examTip":"Influence questions need a clear link from the belief to what Catholics actually do or how they live. Make the development show the effect.","answerTopic":"Trinity – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about the nature of God as Trinity.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics, Orthodox and most Protestants believe God is one being in three co-equal persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, as stated in the Nicene Creed (1 mark). The Son is 'consubstantial with the Father', so Jesus is fully God and not a created being (2nd mark).\n\nSecond, different belief: Some groups that call themselves Christian, such as Unitarians and Jehovah's Witnesses, reject the Trinity (1 mark). Jehovah's Witnesses, for example, believe Jesus is God's first creation and that the Holy Spirit is God's active force rather than a person. They see this as protecting the strict oneness of God taught in Deuteronomy 6:4 (2nd mark).\n\n(Another acceptable difference: Catholics believe the Spirit proceeds from the Father 'and the Son' (filioque), whereas Orthodox Christians believe the Spirit proceeds from the Father alone.)","evidence":["Nicene Creed","Deuteronomy 6:4","Filioque controversy"],"examTip":"'Different' questions need two views that genuinely differ. Two versions of the same Catholic belief will not get full marks.","answerTopic":"Trinity – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Explain two Catholic teachings about the Trinity.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: God is one. Catholics are monotheists and do not believe in three gods (1 mark). The Trinity is 'one God in three persons' (CCC 253). Each person is wholly and entirely God, so the Father, Son and Holy Spirit share one divine nature and work together in creation, salvation and sanctification (2nd mark).\n\nSecond teaching: The three persons are distinct and co-equal (1 mark). The Son is 'eternally begotten of the Father', and the Spirit 'proceeds from the Father and the Son'. None was created and none is lesser, which is why the Council of Nicaea declared the Son 'consubstantial with the Father' against Arius (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: At Jesus's baptism the Son comes up from the water, the Spirit descends like a dove and the Father says 'This is my Son, the Beloved' (Matthew 3:16–17). This shows three distinct persons present and acting at the same moment, which supports the teaching that they are distinct and not just one person in different modes (2 marks: named and applied).","evidence":["CCC 253","Nicene Creed (AD 325/381)","Matthew 3:16–17","Deuteronomy 6:4"],"examTip":"For the 6-mark question, name the source precisely (book, chapter, verse or CCC number) AND say how it supports your point. Naming alone earns only 1 of the 2 source marks.","answerTopic":"Trinity – teachings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"Explain two ways in which the Nicene Creed expresses Catholic belief about the persons of the Trinity.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: The Creed teaches that the Son is fully divine (1 mark). It describes him as 'God from God, Light from Light, true God from true God, begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father'. These phrases were chosen at the Council of Nicaea (325) to reject Arius's claim that the Son was a creature, so Catholics affirm Jesus is equal to the Father (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: The Creed teaches that the Holy Spirit is a divine person (1 mark). It calls the Spirit 'the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son, who with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified'. This was added at Constantinople (381) and means the Spirit is worshipped as God, not seen as just a power or energy (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: John's Gospel opens, 'In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God' (John 1:1). The Creed's language that the Son is 'true God from true God' and existed before all ages draws on this verse. It shows the Son is eternal and divine rather than made (2 marks).","evidence":["Nicene Creed","Council of Nicaea 325; Constantinople 381","John 1:1","John 15:26 (Spirit proceeds from the Father)"],"examTip":"Quoting short phrases from the Creed is good AO1 evidence. The Creed itself counts as a source of Christian belief and teaching.","answerTopic":"Nicene Creed"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"'The Trinity is too difficult to be a helpful way of understanding God.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Trinity is hard to explain. Saying that God is 'one' and also 'three' can sound like a contradiction. Even the Catechism admits that it is a 'mystery' that human reason cannot work out alone (CCC 237). If ordinary believers cannot explain it to a friend, it is fair to ask how it helps them understand God.\n\nThe word 'Trinity' does not appear in the Bible. The precise language of the Creed, such as 'consubstantial' and 'begotten, not made', came from philosophical debates at Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381). Some people argue that this technical language makes God seem distant and academic rather than personal.\n\nAnalogies often fail. St Patrick's shamrock, or water as ice, liquid and steam, can mislead people into modalism, where God is one person changing forms, or tritheism, where God is three separate gods. Jews and Muslims, who also worship one God, find the doctrine confusing and believe it risks undermining God's oneness (Deuteronomy 6:4; tawhid in Islam). Groups such as Jehovah's Witnesses reject it as unnecessary.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholics would reply that a God who could be fully understood would not really be God. The Trinity is difficult because God is greater than our minds. That is a sign of truth, not a weakness. St Augustine is often linked with the saying 'if you understand it, it is not God'.\n\nThe Trinity also explains what Christians actually experience of God in scripture. They meet God as the Father who creates, as Jesus who lives, dies and rises, and as the Holy Spirit poured out at Pentecost (Acts 2). Jesus's baptism (Matthew 3:16–17) and the command to baptise 'in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit' (Matthew 28:19) show all three. Without the Trinity, Christians could not explain how Jesus is both God and distinct from the Father.\n\nThe doctrine is also very practical. It shows that 'God is love' (1 John 4:8), because love requires relationship, and within the Trinity there has always been a loving communion. This encourages Catholics to value community, family and self-giving. It shapes daily prayer through the sign of the cross, the Glory Be and the Creed at Mass. Many ordinary Catholics who could not explain the theology still find it meaningful.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is right that the Trinity is intellectually difficult and that analogies are imperfect. However, it confuses 'difficult to explain' with 'unhelpful'. The stronger argument is that the Trinity, rooted in scripture and defined in the Nicene Creed, holds together the key Christian experiences of God and shows why love lies at the heart of reality. For Catholics, it is the central mystery of faith (CCC 234) and a deeply helpful way of understanding God, even though it is never fully understood.","evidence":["CCC 234, 237","Matthew 3:16–17; Matthew 28:19","1 John 4:8","Deuteronomy 6:4","Councils of Nicaea and Constantinople","Analogies: shamrock, water"],"examTip":"In the 12-mark question, build chains of reasoning: point, then evidence, then explanation of why it matters for the statement. Your conclusion should weigh the arguments, not just repeat them.","answerTopic":"Trinity – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","question":"'The Holy Spirit is the most important person of the Trinity for Catholics today.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Holy Spirit is the person of the Trinity whom Catholics experience most directly in daily life. Jesus promised that the Father would send 'the Advocate, the Holy Spirit' to 'teach you everything' (John 14:26). At Pentecost the Spirit came upon the apostles and gave them courage to preach (Acts 2), so the Church itself began through the Spirit. Catholics today believe the Spirit guides the Pope and bishops and keeps the Church in the truth.\n\nThe Spirit is active in the sacraments that Catholics receive. In Baptism they are born 'of water and Spirit' (John 3:5). In Confirmation they receive the seven gifts of the Spirit, such as wisdom and courage. At every Mass the priest calls down the Spirit on the bread and wine (the epiclesis). In this sense the Spirit is the one who makes God present now, which could make the Spirit seem the most important person for Catholics living today.\n\nCharismatic Catholics, a movement that grew from 1967 onwards, place special emphasis on the Spirit's gifts, such as prayer in tongues and healing. For them, a living relationship with the Spirit is central to faith.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching says the three persons are co-equal. The Nicene Creed says the Spirit is 'with the Father and the Son… adored and glorified'. Calling any one person 'most important' risks misunderstanding the Trinity, because each person is fully God and they always act together (CCC 258). The question itself can be seen as based on a mistake.\n\nOthers might argue that the Son, Jesus, is the most important for Catholics. Jesus is the one through whom God is fully revealed. He is the one whose death and resurrection bring salvation, and the one whose Body and Blood Catholics receive in the Eucharist, the 'source and summit' of Christian life. Christian moral life is about following Jesus's example. Many Catholics pray most naturally to Jesus, for example in devotion to the Sacred Heart.\n\nStill others would emphasise the Father as creator and source of the Trinity. Jesus taught his followers to pray 'Our Father' (Matthew 6:9), and the Mass prayers are mostly addressed to the Father, through the Son, in the Spirit.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement has some strength in practice. The Spirit is how Catholics experience God's presence in the Church, the sacraments and their consciences today. However, the most convincing view is that, in Catholic teaching, no person of the Trinity is more important than another. Catholic prayer is directed to the Father, through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. Each person plays a distinct role, but all are equally God. So the statement is best rejected, while recognising that some Catholics feel particularly close to the Spirit.","evidence":["John 14:26","Acts 2 (Pentecost)","John 3:5","Nicene Creed","CCC 258","Matthew 6:9","Seven gifts of the Spirit"],"examTip":"Questions that ask which is 'most important' often have a trap. Here, Catholic teaching on co-equality is the key counter-argument. Using it shows precise knowledge.","answerTopic":"Holy Spirit – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"In Genesis 2, from what does God form the first man?\nA) Water from a river\nB) A rib\nC) The dust of the ground\nD) Light","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) The dust of the ground.\n\nGenesis 2:7: 'the LORD God formed man from the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life'. The rib (B) is used to form the woman (Genesis 2:21–22).","evidence":["Genesis 2:7","Genesis 2:21–22"],"examTip":"Know the details of both accounts. Examiners can test the difference between Genesis 1 (word, six days) and Genesis 2 (dust, breath, garden, rib).","answerTopic":"Genesis 2"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Which term means that God created the universe 'out of nothing'?\nA) Ex nihilo\nB) Imago Dei\nC) Agape\nD) Magisterium","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: A) Ex nihilo.\n\nEx nihilo is Latin for 'out of nothing' (CCC 296). Imago Dei means 'image of God'. Agape is selfless love. The Magisterium is the teaching authority of the Church.","evidence":["CCC 296","Ex nihilo"],"examTip":"Latin key terms come up often. Make a glossary card for each one.","answerTopic":"Creation ex nihilo"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Give one thing that the Genesis creation accounts show about the nature of God.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• God is omnipotent (creates simply by speaking)\n• God is creator of everything / creates out of nothing\n• God is good (everything was 'very good')\n• God is transcendent, beyond and before creation\n• God is personal or immanent (breathes life into Adam, walks in the garden)\n• God is a God of order","evidence":["Genesis 1:3, 1:31","Genesis 2:7"],"examTip":"One clear attribute is enough. 'Omnipotent' or 'creator' is fine.","answerTopic":"Nature of God in creation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Give one meaning of the Catholic belief that humans are made 'in the image of God'.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Every human being has dignity and sacred value\n• Humans have reason and free will\n• Humans have a soul / can have a relationship with God\n• Humans can love and be creative\n• Male and female are equal in dignity (Genesis 1:27)","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","CCC 357"],"examTip":"Avoid saying humans 'look like' God physically. Catholic teaching is about spiritual qualities and dignity.","answerTopic":"Imago Dei"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Explain two ways in which belief that humans are made in the image of God influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics believe every human life is sacred from conception to natural death (1 mark). Because every person reflects God's image, Catholics oppose abortion and euthanasia, and many support hospices and pregnancy-support charities (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Catholics treat all people as equal in dignity regardless of race, sex, wealth or disability (1 mark). Genesis 1:27 says God made humans 'male and female', both in his image. This motivates Catholics to challenge discrimination and to support refugees and the poor through groups such as CAFOD and the SVP. CST teaches that human dignity is the foundation of all social justice (2nd mark).","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Sanctity of life","Catholic social teaching: human dignity"],"examTip":"Make each development show what Catholics do as a result of the belief. That is what 'influence' means.","answerTopic":"Imago Dei – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Explain two different Christian views about how the Genesis creation accounts should be understood.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Some Christians, such as fundamentalist evangelicals or 'Young Earth' creationists, read Genesis literally (1 mark). They believe God created the world in six 24-hour days only a few thousand years ago. They reject evolution because the Bible is the inerrant word of God and should be taken at face value (2nd mark).\n\nSecond view: The Catholic Church teaches that Genesis uses symbolic or figurative language to express religious truths (1 mark). It is true about who created the world and why, not about how or when. Catholics can therefore accept the Big Bang (first proposed by the Catholic priest Georges Lemaître) and evolution, provided God is understood as creator and the human soul as directly given by God (CCC 390; Humani Generis) (2nd mark).","evidence":["CCC 390","Humani Generis (1950)","Georges Lemaître","Young Earth creationism"],"examTip":"Make sure the two views genuinely differ, for example literal versus symbolic, and develop each one with a reason or example.","answerTopic":"Interpreting Genesis"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the role of human beings in creation.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Humans are stewards of creation (1 mark). God put Adam in the garden 'to till it and keep it' (Genesis 2:15), so humans are responsible to God for caring for the earth. They do not own it outright, and they will be accountable for how they treat the environment and animals (CCC 2415) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Humans are the high point of creation and have a unique dignity (1 mark). They were created last, after God said 'Let us make humankind in our image' (Genesis 1:26). Only humans have a rational soul and can know and love God, so they are called to relationship with God and with each other (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: In Laudato Si' (2015), Pope Francis teaches that 'dominion' in Genesis 1:28 does not justify exploitation. He calls Catholics to an 'ecological conversion' to care for 'our common home'. This applies the belief in stewardship directly, so Catholics today should reduce waste and act on climate change (2 marks).","evidence":["Genesis 1:26–28","Genesis 2:15","CCC 2415","Laudato Si' (2015)"],"examTip":"A papal encyclical is a valid 'source of Christian belief and teaching'. Give its name and apply what it says to the question.","answerTopic":"Stewardship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"Explain two things that the Genesis creation accounts teach Catholics about God.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: God is omnipotent and transcendent (1 mark). In Genesis 1 God creates simply by speaking: 'Let there be light'. He exists before and beyond everything he creates, which Catholics express as creation ex nihilo, out of nothing (CCC 296) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond teaching: God is personal, loving and immanent (1 mark). In Genesis 2 God shapes the man from dust and 'breathed into his nostrils the breath of life' (Genesis 2:7). He provides a garden and a companion, which shows that God is closely involved with his creation and cares for human needs (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Genesis 1:31 says 'God saw everything that he had made, and indeed, it was very good'. This teaches that God is good and that the material world is not evil. Catholics therefore see creation as a gift to be valued and cared for (2 marks).","evidence":["Genesis 1:3","Genesis 1:31","Genesis 2:7","CCC 296"],"examTip":"Precise quotations from Genesis are easy evidence to learn. Short phrases are enough.","answerTopic":"God as creator"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"'The Genesis creation stories cannot be true if science explains the origin of the universe.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nScientific evidence suggests the universe began around 13.8 billion years ago in the Big Bang, and that life developed over billions of years through evolution by natural selection. Genesis 1 describes the world being made in six days, with plants appearing before the sun. If Genesis is read as a factual account, it clashes with what science has found.\n\nSome atheists, such as Richard Dawkins, argue that once science gives a complete natural explanation, there is no need for God. On this view, the creation stories are pre-scientific myths and so are not 'true'.\n\nSome Christians agree that the two cannot both be right, but they reject the science. Young Earth creationists believe the Bible is inerrant and must be literally true, so they reject evolution and an ancient universe. Both they and many atheists assume that the Bible and science are rivals.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic Church teaches that there is no real conflict, because science and Genesis answer different questions. Science explains how the universe developed. Genesis explains who created it and why. The Catechism says Genesis expresses the truth of creation in 'figurative language' (CCC 390). The truths it teaches are that God is creator, that creation is good and that humans have dignity, and none of these is contradicted by physics or biology.\n\nCatholic tradition values science. The Big Bang theory was first proposed by Fr Georges Lemaître, a Catholic priest. Pope John Paul II said in 1996 that evolution is 'more than a hypothesis'. The Pontifical Academy of Sciences includes leading scientists. Pope Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) allowed Catholics to accept evolution of the body, provided they believe God directly creates each human soul.\n\nScience may even point towards God. Some argue that the Big Bang raises the question of what caused it, which links to the cosmological argument (Aquinas's 'first cause'). The fine-tuning of the universe for life suggests design. On this view, science describes the method God used.\n\nA story can also be 'true' without being literal, in the way a parable is true. Genesis teaches that the world is ordered, good and created in love, and that humans are stewards (Genesis 2:15). These are truths about meaning and purpose that science cannot test.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement only works if Genesis is understood as a scientific report. If it is read literally, there is a genuine conflict. But the Catholic Church does not read it that way. It sees faith and reason as 'two wings' (John Paul II, Fides et Ratio). Since science explains processes while Genesis explains meaning and purpose, the more convincing conclusion is that Genesis can be true in the ways that matter most, even if science explains how the universe began.","evidence":["CCC 390","Humani Generis (1950)","John Paul II, 1996 address on evolution; Fides et Ratio (1998)","Georges Lemaître","Genesis 1; Genesis 2:15","Cosmological argument"],"examTip":"Avoid presenting this as 'religion versus science'. The best answers show that Christians themselves disagree (literalist versus Catholic) and explain why.","answerTopic":"Creation and science"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","question":"'Humans have a duty to protect the environment above everything else.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nGenesis teaches that God placed humans in the garden 'to till it and keep it' (Genesis 2:15). Humans are therefore stewards who must answer to God for how they treat creation. If the earth is damaged beyond repair by climate change, pollution and loss of species, every other human activity is threatened. Some would say protecting it must come first.\n\nPope Francis's Laudato Si' (2015) speaks of an urgent ecological crisis. It warns that the earth 'is beginning to look more and more like an immense pile of filth'. It calls all people to 'ecological conversion'. The Catechism says humans may not use animals and nature without regard for the 'integrity of creation' (CCC 2415). Since the poorest people suffer most from environmental damage, protecting the environment is also a matter of justice.\n\nMany non-religious people, including environmental activists, argue that the survival of the planet is the most basic priority. They see humans as part of nature, not above it.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching does not put the environment 'above everything else'. Humans alone are made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), so human life and dignity have a unique value. Laudato Si' rejects a 'biocentrism' that treats humans as just one species among many. It teaches 'integral ecology': care for nature must go together with care for the poor, the unborn and the vulnerable. Protecting the environment while ignoring human suffering would be a distortion.\n\nThe greatest commandment is to love God, and the second is to love one's neighbour (Matthew 22:37–39). For a Catholic, the first duty is to God. Caring for creation is one way of loving God and neighbour, not something higher than them. Treating nature as the highest value could even become a form of idolatry.\n\nThere are also practical trade-offs. Poor communities may need development, such as roads, electricity and farming, to escape poverty. Catholic agencies such as CAFOD try to balance sustainability with people's right to a decent life (Gaudium et Spes 26).\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is right that care for creation is a serious, God-given duty and that the environmental crisis is urgent. But the words 'above everything else' go too far from a Catholic perspective. The most convincing view is that environmental protection is part of a wider duty: to love God, respect human dignity and seek the common good. As Laudato Si' puts it, 'everything is connected'. Humans should protect the environment as an expression of love for God and neighbour, not instead of it.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27; 2:15","Laudato Si' (2015): ecological conversion, integral ecology","CCC 2415","Matthew 22:37–39","Gaudium et Spes 26"],"examTip":"Words like 'above everything else' are what you are being asked to judge. Make sure your conclusion addresses them directly.","answerTopic":"Stewardship – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Which one of the following phrases from John's Gospel refers to the Incarnation?\nA) 'I am the bread of life'\nB) 'The Word became flesh and lived among us'\nC) 'It is finished'\nD) 'Peace be with you'","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) 'The Word became flesh and lived among us' (John 1:14).\n\nThis verse describes the eternal Word (the Son) taking human nature. A is an 'I am' saying about the Eucharist (John 6:35). C is Jesus's last word on the cross (John 19:30). D is the risen Jesus's greeting (John 20:19).","evidence":["John 1:14"],"examTip":"Learn John 1:1 and 1:14 word-for-word. They are the key texts for the Incarnation.","answerTopic":"Word made flesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Which title, confirmed at the Council of Ephesus, means Mary is the 'God-bearer' or 'Mother of God'?\nA) Messiah\nB) Logos\nC) Theotokos\nD) Paraclete","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) Theotokos.\n\nTheotokos ('God-bearer') was affirmed at Ephesus in 431 because the child Mary bore is the divine Son. Messiah means 'anointed one' (Jesus). Logos means 'Word' (the Son). Paraclete means 'Advocate' (the Holy Spirit).","evidence":["Council of Ephesus 431","CCC 495"],"examTip":"Titles of Mary are about who Jesus is. Theotokos protects the belief that Jesus is truly God.","answerTopic":"Mary, Mother of God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Give one piece of evidence from the Gospels that Jesus was fully human.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• He was born as a baby to Mary (Luke 2:7)\n• He wept at Lazarus's tomb (John 11:35)\n• He was hungry after fasting / tempted in the desert (Matthew 4:2)\n• He felt anguish and sweated in Gethsemane (Luke 22:44)\n• He was thirsty and died on the cross (John 19:28–30)","evidence":["John 11:35","Matthew 4:2","Luke 22:44"],"examTip":"Pick one clear example. A Bible reference makes the answer stronger, but it is not required for 1 mark.","answerTopic":"Humanity of Jesus"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Give one meaning of the name 'Emmanuel'.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"God is with us (Matthew 1:23, quoting Isaiah 7:14).","evidence":["Matthew 1:23","Isaiah 7:14"],"examTip":"Short 'Give' answers only need the meaning. 'God with us' is enough.","answerTopic":"Emmanuel"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Incarnation influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics celebrate the Incarnation in worship (1 mark). At Christmas they attend Midnight Mass and set up cribs (nativity scenes), a custom linked to St Francis of Assisi. During the Creed they bow at the words 'by the Holy Spirit was incarnate of the Virgin Mary, and became man', showing reverence for God becoming human (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Because God became human, Catholics believe human life and the physical world are holy (1 mark). Jesus shared ordinary human life, so Catholics believe God can be met in other people, especially the poor (Matthew 25:40). This inspires charity work and respect for the dignity of every human being (2nd mark).","evidence":["Nicene Creed","Matthew 25:40","Christmas Midnight Mass"],"examTip":"Use real practices (bowing in the Creed, Christmas, the Angelus) to show influence.","answerTopic":"Incarnation – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about the nature of Jesus.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics, along with Orthodox and most Protestant Christians, believe Jesus is one person who is fully God and fully human (1 mark). The Council of Chalcedon (451) taught that his two natures are united 'without confusion, change, division or separation', so Jesus could both suffer as a human and save as God (2nd mark).\n\nSecond, different belief: Some liberal Christians emphasise Jesus's humanity (1 mark). They see him as a man uniquely open to God, and see stories such as the virgin birth as symbolic expressions of his importance rather than literal biology. For them, Jesus's moral teaching and example matter more than metaphysical claims about divinity (2nd mark).","evidence":["Council of Chalcedon 451","Liberal Christian Christology"],"examTip":"Two genuinely different views are needed. One traditional and one liberal view is a safe pairing.","answerTopic":"Christology – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the Incarnation.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Jesus is the eternal Son of God who took on human nature (1 mark). He did not begin to exist at his birth. As the second person of the Trinity he existed from eternity and 'came down from heaven', as the Nicene Creed says, becoming man without ceasing to be God (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Jesus was conceived by the power of the Holy Spirit and born of the Virgin Mary (1 mark). The angel tells Mary, 'The Holy Spirit will come upon you… the child to be born will be holy; he will be called Son of God' (Luke 1:35). This shows his conception was a unique act of God (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: John 1:1 says 'the Word was God', and 1:14 says 'the Word became flesh and lived among us'. Together these verses show that the divine Word, who was God, truly became human. That is the core of the Catholic belief that Jesus is fully God and fully human (2 marks).","evidence":["John 1:1, 14","Luke 1:35","Nicene Creed","CCC 464"],"examTip":"Two distinct beliefs, for example 'eternal Son becomes human' and 'virgin birth', are safer than two versions of the same idea.","answerTopic":"Incarnation – beliefs"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"Explain two reasons why the Incarnation is important for Catholics.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: The Incarnation makes salvation possible (1 mark). Catholics believe only someone who is both God and human could restore the broken relationship between God and humanity. As a human, Jesus could offer his life on our behalf. As God, his sacrifice has infinite value (CCC 457) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: In Jesus, God reveals himself fully and personally (1 mark). People no longer have to guess what God is like. They see God's love in Jesus's words, healing and forgiveness, and he is a perfect model of how to live (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Jesus says to Philip, 'Whoever has seen me has seen the Father' (John 14:9). Through the Incarnation, God the Father can be known by looking at Jesus. This supports the belief that the Incarnation is God's fullest revelation of himself (2 marks).","evidence":["John 14:9","CCC 457–460","Matthew 1:21 'he will save his people from their sins'","Colossians 1:15"],"examTip":"'Why important' questions need reasons, not just a description. Start each point with 'because…'.","answerTopic":"Importance of the Incarnation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"'Jesus was just a good man.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMost historians agree that Jesus was a real Jewish teacher who lived in first-century Palestine and was crucified under Pontius Pilate. His teaching, such as 'love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44) and the parable of the Good Samaritan, is admired by people of all faiths and none. A humanist may accept that Jesus was an outstanding moral example while seeing claims about divinity as later additions by his followers. Such claims cannot be scientifically proven.\n\nOther religions also honour Jesus without calling him God. Judaism sees him as a teacher but not the Messiah. Islam reveres Isa as a great prophet born of a virgin, but teaches that God has no son. Some liberal Christians focus on Jesus's humanity and see the miracle stories and virgin birth as symbolic.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching firmly rejects the idea that Jesus was only a good man. The Nicene Creed calls him 'true God from true God… consubstantial with the Father'. The Church teaches the Incarnation: the eternal Word 'became flesh' (John 1:14) in Jesus, who is fully God and fully human (Council of Chalcedon, 451; CCC 464).\n\nThe Gospels present Jesus doing things only God can do. He forgives sins, and the onlookers ask, 'Who can forgive sins but God alone?' (Mark 2:7). He calms storms, raises the dead and rises from the dead himself. He says 'The Father and I are one' (John 10:30), and Thomas worships him as 'My Lord and my God!' (John 20:28).\n\nC. S. Lewis's well-known argument (the 'trilemma') says that someone who claimed to be God cannot be 'just a good man'. Either he was lying (bad), deluded (mad), or telling the truth (God). On this view, 'just a good man' is the one option that does not fit the evidence of his claims.\n\nThe resurrection is central here. St Paul writes that 'if Christ has not been raised, your faith has been in vain' (1 Corinthians 15:17). The rapid growth of the early Church, and apostles willing to die for their belief, suggest that they experienced something more than a good man's death.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is reasonable from a non-religious or non-Christian point of view, which accepts Jesus's goodness but not his divinity. But for Catholics it misses what the Gospels and the Church actually claim. Jesus's authority to forgive sins, his resurrection and the Church's consistent teaching from Nicaea to today point to the belief that he is God incarnate. Since the 'good man' view ignores Jesus's own claims about himself, the stronger argument is that he was more than a good man, though this remains a matter of faith rather than proof.","evidence":["John 1:14","Mark 2:7","John 10:30; 20:28","1 Corinthians 15:17","Council of Chalcedon; Nicene Creed","C. S. Lewis's trilemma","Islam: Isa as prophet"],"examTip":"Including non-religious and other-faith views strengthens a 12-mark answer, but make sure Catholic teaching is clearly explained.","answerTopic":"Divinity of Jesus – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","question":"'The Incarnation is the most important Catholic belief.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nWithout the Incarnation, almost every other Christian belief would collapse. If the Word had not 'become flesh' (John 1:14), there would be no life of Jesus, no crucifixion, no resurrection and no Eucharist. Salvation depends on Jesus being both God and man. As man he could offer himself for humanity, and as God his sacrifice could reconcile the whole world with God (CCC 457). In this sense the Incarnation is the foundation on which the rest is built.\n\nThe Incarnation also reveals who God is. Jesus said, 'Whoever has seen me has seen the Father' (John 14:9). In the Incarnation, the God who is beyond all things becomes Emmanuel, 'God with us' (Matthew 1:23). It also explains why Catholics treat physical things such as bread, wine, water and oil as channels of grace in the sacraments. Because God took flesh, matter can carry the divine.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catechism calls the Trinity 'the central mystery of Christian faith and life… the source of all the other mysteries' (CCC 234). Without the Trinity there would be no eternal Son to become incarnate. Many Catholics would therefore say the Trinity is the more fundamental belief.\n\nOthers would point to the resurrection. St Paul wrote, 'If Christ has not been raised, your faith has been in vain' (1 Corinthians 15:17). The Church calls Easter the 'feast of feasts' and celebrates it every Sunday. It is the resurrection that proves Jesus's victory over sin and death and gives Catholics hope of eternal life.\n\nIn practical terms, many Catholics might say the Eucharist matters most, because it is 'the source and summit of the Christian life' (Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324). It is how they meet Christ each week. Some theologians also argue that it is unhelpful to rank beliefs at all. Catholic doctrines form a connected whole (the 'hierarchy of truths' links each truth to the central mystery of Christ).\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe Incarnation is clearly essential. Salvation, revelation and the sacramental life all depend on it. However, calling it the most important belief is hard to justify when the Church itself names the Trinity as the central mystery and the resurrection as the high point of the liturgical year. The most convincing conclusion is that the Incarnation is one of a few core beliefs, alongside the Trinity and the resurrection, that cannot be separated. Each makes sense only in the light of the others.","evidence":["John 1:14; 14:9","Matthew 1:23","CCC 234, 457, 1324","1 Corinthians 15:17","Lumen Gentium 11"],"examTip":"When asked whether something is 'most important', compare it with named alternatives (here the Trinity, the resurrection and the Eucharist) and give reasons for each.","answerTopic":"Incarnation – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Which word means 'the free and undeserved gift of God's love and help'?\nA) Atonement\nB) Grace\nC) Covenant\nD) Repentance","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Grace.\n\nThe Catechism defines grace as 'favour, the free and undeserved help that God gives us' (CCC 1996). Atonement is reconciliation through Christ's sacrifice. A covenant is a binding agreement. Repentance is turning away from sin.","evidence":["CCC 1996"],"examTip":"Learn short definitions of the key terms: grace, atonement, redemption, salvation, sin.","answerTopic":"Grace"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Which event, described in Acts 1, happened forty days after the resurrection?\nA) Pentecost\nB) The Transfiguration\nC) The Ascension\nD) The Last Supper","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) The Ascension.\n\nActs 1:3–11 describes Jesus appearing for forty days and then being 'lifted up' to heaven. Pentecost came ten days later (Acts 2). The Transfiguration and the Last Supper were before his death.","evidence":["Acts 1:3–11"],"examTip":"Get the order right: Last Supper, crucifixion, resurrection, ascension (40 days), Pentecost (50 days).","answerTopic":"Ascension"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Give one reason why Catholics believe humans need redemption.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Because of original sin / the Fall of Adam and Eve (Genesis 3)\n• Sin separates humans from God\n• Humans cannot save themselves by their own efforts\n• The 'wages of sin is death' (Romans 6:23)","evidence":["Genesis 3","Romans 6:23","CCC 402"],"examTip":"One clear reason only. 'Original sin' is a precise answer.","answerTopic":"Need for redemption"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Give one way in which Catholics believe they receive God's grace.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Through the sacraments (e.g. Baptism, the Eucharist, Reconciliation)\n• Through prayer\n• Through faith in Jesus Christ\n• Through reading scripture / the Word of God","evidence":["CCC 1131","CCC 2003"],"examTip":"'The sacraments' or one named sacrament is the most precise answer.","answerTopic":"Grace"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the resurrection of Jesus influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics celebrate the resurrection in worship (1 mark). Easter is the most important festival, centred on the Easter Vigil with the lighting of the Paschal candle. Every Sunday is a 'little Easter', which is why Catholics have a duty to attend Mass on Sundays (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: The resurrection gives Catholics hope in the face of death (1 mark). Because Christ rose, they believe they too will be raised (1 Corinthians 15:20–22). Funerals are therefore times of hope, with the Paschal candle beside the coffin, and Catholics may face suffering and grief with trust in eternal life (2nd mark).","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:20–22","Easter Vigil, Paschal candle","Sunday as the Lord's Day"],"examTip":"Make the link clear: 'Because they believe Jesus rose… they…'.","answerTopic":"Resurrection – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about how people are saved.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: The Catholic Church teaches that salvation is a free gift of God's grace, received through faith and expressed in good works and the sacraments (1 mark). Baptism begins the life of grace. Catholics are called to cooperate with grace by loving God and neighbour, since 'faith by itself, if it has no works, is dead' (James 2:17) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Many Protestant Christians, following Martin Luther, teach justification by faith alone (sola fide) (1 mark). They believe a person is put right with God simply by trusting in Christ's death, not by anything they do. Good works are the result of salvation, not a means to it, based on Ephesians 2:8–9: 'by grace you have been saved through faith… not the result of works' (2nd mark).","evidence":["James 2:17","Ephesians 2:8–9","Sola fide (Luther)","Joint Declaration on Justification (1999)"],"examTip":"Name the group holding each view (Catholic or Protestant) and give the scriptural reason behind it.","answerTopic":"Salvation – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the significance of Jesus's death.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Jesus's death is a sacrifice that atones for the sins of humanity (1 mark). Jesus freely offered his life in place of sinners, taking on the consequences of sin so that people could be forgiven and reconciled with God. That is why Catholics call him the Lamb of God (John 1:29) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Jesus's death shows the depth of God's love (1 mark). God was willing to give up his own Son rather than leave humanity lost. Catholics are called to respond with love and self-sacrifice of their own, 'taking up their cross' (Luke 9:23) (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Jesus says the Son of Man came 'to give his life a ransom for many' (Mark 10:45). This shows that he understood his death as freeing people from sin, in the way a ransom frees a captive. That is the meaning of redemption, 'buying back' (2 marks).","evidence":["Mark 10:45","John 1:29","John 3:16","CCC 613–617"],"examTip":"Use the language of sacrifice, ransom and atonement accurately. These are specialist terms that earn credit.","answerTopic":"Death of Jesus"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the ascension of Jesus.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: The ascension marks Jesus's return to the Father in glory (1 mark). His mission on earth was complete. In his risen body he entered God's presence, and he now 'sits at the right hand of the Father', as the Nicene Creed says, reigning as Lord and interceding for humanity (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: The ascension opens heaven to humanity and begins the mission of the Church (1 mark). Because Jesus took his human nature into heaven, Catholics hope to follow him (CCC 661). Before ascending, he commanded the apostles to be his 'witnesses… to the ends of the earth', and he promised the Holy Spirit (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: In Acts 1:9–11 Jesus is 'lifted up, and a cloud took him out of their sight'. Angels then say he 'will come in the same way'. This teaches that the ascension is not the end of the story: Christ will return at the Second Coming, so Catholics live in hope and readiness (2 marks).","evidence":["Acts 1:8–11","Nicene Creed","CCC 659–667"],"examTip":"The ascension is often overlooked. Learn what it means (return to glory, heaven opened, mission and Spirit, Second Coming).","answerTopic":"Ascension"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"'The resurrection is more important than the crucifixion for Catholics.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nSt Paul says that 'if Christ has not been raised, your faith has been in vain' (1 Corinthians 15:17). Many people were crucified by the Romans. What makes Jesus unique, according to Christians, is that he rose from the dead. The resurrection proves he is the Son of God, shows God's approval of his sacrifice and reveals his victory over death.\n\nThe resurrection is also the source of Catholic hope. Because Christ rose, believers will be raised too (1 Corinthians 15:20–22). This is why Catholic funerals are hopeful, with the white pall and Paschal candle. The Church's calendar reflects the importance of the resurrection. Easter is the 'feast of feasts', and Sunday, the day of resurrection, is the Lord's Day on which Catholics must attend Mass.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching sees Jesus's death as the act of redemption itself. Jesus came 'to give his life a ransom for many' (Mark 10:45), and his sacrifice atones for the sin of the world. Without the crucifixion there would be no sacrifice, no forgiveness and nothing to rise from. The two events form one Paschal Mystery (CCC 571) and cannot be separated.\n\nThe crucifixion is at the centre of Catholic worship. The Mass makes present the one sacrifice of Calvary (CCC 1366). The crucifix, showing Christ's body, hangs in every Catholic church, whereas many Protestant churches use an empty cross. Catholics pray the Stations of the Cross and the Sorrowful Mysteries of the Rosary. They venerate the cross on Good Friday. This all suggests the crucifixion is at least as important as the resurrection.\n\nThe crucifixion also speaks powerfully to suffering people. It shows a God who shares human pain. Catholics are called to 'take up their cross' (Luke 9:23), and many find in Christ's suffering a meaning for their own.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThere are strong reasons on both sides. The resurrection confirms Jesus's identity and gives hope, while the crucifixion is the sacrifice that brings redemption. The most convincing conclusion, and the one closest to Catholic teaching, is that neither is more important. They are two parts of one saving event. The cross without the resurrection would be a tragedy, and the resurrection without the cross would have no sacrifice for sin. That is why the Church celebrates them together in the Easter Triduum, from Good Friday to Easter Sunday.","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:17, 20–22","Mark 10:45","Luke 9:23","CCC 571, 1366","Easter Triduum","Crucifix versus empty cross"],"examTip":"When two things are linked, it is a strong evaluative move to argue that they are inseparable. Do still give real arguments for each side first.","answerTopic":"Crucifixion and resurrection"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","question":"'Doing good works is the only way to be saved.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nJesus's parable of the sheep and the goats (Matthew 25:31–46) suggests that people are judged by what they have done. Feeding the hungry, welcoming strangers and visiting prisoners lead to eternal life, and failing to do these leads to punishment. 'Just as you did it to one of the least of these… you did it to me' (25:40). James 2:17 says that faith 'by itself, if it has no works, is dead'.\n\nSome people argue that a good God would not exclude someone who lived a genuinely loving life simply because they did not belong to a particular faith. Many non-religious people believe goodness is what matters most, and that a just God (if one exists) would reward it.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching says salvation is first of all God's gift, not something we earn. Grace is 'free and undeserved' (CCC 1996). No one can save themselves from sin. Only Jesus's death and resurrection redeem humanity, and 'there is salvation in no one else' (Acts 4:12). Good works are important, but they are a response to grace and done with its help, not a way of buying our way into heaven. So 'the only way' is wrong.\n\nThe sacraments and faith are also part of the way to salvation. Jesus says, 'no one can enter the kingdom of God without being born of water and Spirit' (John 3:5), which Catholics apply to Baptism. Faith in Christ, repentance and the sacraments of Reconciliation and the Eucharist are means by which God's saving grace is received.\n\nProtestant Christians go further. Following Luther and Ephesians 2:8–9 ('by grace you have been saved through faith… not the result of works'), they teach justification by faith alone. For them, works are the fruit of salvation, never its cause.\n\nAt the same time, the Catholic Church teaches that those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ, but who seek God sincerely and try to do his will through conscience, 'may achieve eternal salvation' (Lumen Gentium 16). Even here, salvation comes through Christ's grace working in them.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nGood works clearly matter. Matthew 25 and James show that faith without love is empty, and Catholics reject a faith that makes no difference to how one lives. But the claim that good works are the only way to be saved contradicts Catholic teaching that salvation is God's gift of grace through Christ. The more convincing view is that salvation comes through grace, received in faith and the sacraments and lived out in good works. Works are necessary as the response to grace, but they are not the only or the first thing.","evidence":["Matthew 25:31–46","James 2:17","Ephesians 2:8–9","Acts 4:12","John 3:5","CCC 1996","Lumen Gentium 16"],"examTip":"Watch for absolute words like 'only'. They often let you argue for a balanced 'both… and' conclusion.","answerTopic":"Salvation – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"What is the Catholic name for the state of purification after death for those who die in God's friendship but are not yet completely purified?\nA) Limbo\nB) Purgatory\nC) Paradise\nD) Parousia","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Purgatory.\n\nCCC 1030–1031 describes purgatory as 'final purification of the elect'. Limbo was a theological theory about unbaptised infants, never defined doctrine. Paradise usually means heaven. Parousia is Christ's Second Coming.","evidence":["CCC 1030–1031"],"examTip":"Watch for distractors with similar-sounding terms (Paradise or Parousia).","answerTopic":"Purgatory"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Which parable of Jesus teaches that people will be judged on how they treated the hungry, strangers, sick and prisoners?\nA) The Prodigal Son\nB) The Sheep and the Goats\nC) The Sower\nD) The Mustard Seed","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) The Sheep and the Goats (Matthew 25:31–46).\n\nThe King separates people according to whether they helped 'the least of these'. The Prodigal Son is about forgiveness. The Sower and the Mustard Seed are about the kingdom of God.","evidence":["Matthew 25:31–46"],"examTip":"Matthew 25 is useful in many topics: judgement, love of neighbour, the SVP and CAFOD. Learn it well.","answerTopic":"Judgement"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Give one Catholic belief about heaven.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Eternal life in perfect union with God\n• Seeing God face to face (the beatific vision)\n• Being with Mary, the angels and the saints\n• A state of perfect happiness / fulfilment\n• Only those who die in God's grace and are fully purified enter heaven","evidence":["CCC 1023–1029","1 Corinthians 13:12"],"examTip":"One accurate belief is enough. 'Seeing God face to face' is a strong, precise answer.","answerTopic":"Heaven"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Give one Catholic practice linked to belief in purgatory.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Praying for the dead\n• Having a Mass offered for a dead person (Mass intention)\n• Keeping All Souls' Day (2 November)\n• Praying the Rosary or other prayers for the souls in purgatory\n• Giving alms / doing good works offered for the dead","evidence":["2 Maccabees 12:46","CCC 1032"],"examTip":"Practices linked to beliefs are a favourite AQA question. Always connect them in revision.","answerTopic":"Purgatory"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in judgement influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics try to live moral lives and avoid sin because they will be held accountable to God (1 mark). They examine their conscience and go to the sacrament of Reconciliation, so that they are prepared for the particular judgement at death (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Catholics help people in need because Jesus taught that people will be judged by how they treat 'the least of these' (Matthew 25:40) (1 mark). This motivates them to volunteer with groups like the SVP, give to CAFOD, visit the sick and help at food banks. In serving the poor they are serving Christ (2nd mark).","evidence":["Matthew 25:31–46","Sacrament of Reconciliation"],"examTip":"Two different influences, one 'religious practice' and one 'moral action', is a reliable structure.","answerTopic":"Judgement – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about life after death.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics believe in purgatory, a state of purification for those who die in God's grace but still need cleansing before heaven (1 mark). They therefore pray for the dead and have Masses said for them, believing those prayers can help souls on their way to heaven (2 Maccabees 12:46; CCC 1032) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Most Protestant Christians reject purgatory (1 mark). They believe that, through faith in Christ's complete sacrifice, a believer goes directly to be with God at death. They see no biblical basis for purgatory, partly because 2 Maccabees is not in the Protestant Bible, so they do not pray for the dead in the same way (2nd mark).","evidence":["CCC 1030–1032","2 Maccabees 12:46","Protestant canon"],"examTip":"Purgatory is the clearest Catholic–Protestant difference on this topic. It is a reliable choice for a 'different views' question.","answerTopic":"Afterlife – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the resurrection of the dead.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics believe in the resurrection of the body, not just the survival of the soul (1 mark). At death the soul is separated from the body. At the end of time God will reunite each soul with a glorified, transformed body, as the Creed says: 'I look forward to the resurrection of the dead' (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: The resurrection of the dead is made possible by Jesus's own resurrection (1 mark). Christ is 'the first fruits' of those who have died. His risen body is the model of the resurrected body: real and recognisable, but no longer subject to death or decay (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: In 1 Corinthians 15:42–44 St Paul writes that what is 'sown a physical body' is 'raised a spiritual body', 'perishable' becoming 'imperishable'. This teaches that the resurrected body will be the same person, but transformed and glorified (2 marks).","evidence":["1 Corinthians 15:20, 42–44","Nicene Creed","CCC 997–1001","Luke 24:39–43"],"examTip":"Bodily resurrection is distinctively Christian. Contrast it clearly with the soul simply living on.","answerTopic":"Resurrection of the dead"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about hell.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Hell is eternal separation from God (1 mark). Its chief punishment is the loss of God, for whom every human being was created and in whom alone they can find happiness (CCC 1035) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Hell is a free choice, not something God forces on people (1 mark). God 'predestines no one' to hell (CCC 1037). Those who end there have freely and finally rejected God's love by dying in unrepented mortal sin, so hell respects human free will (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: In the parable of the sheep and the goats, the King says to those who failed to help the needy, 'depart from me… into the eternal fire' (Matthew 25:41). This shows that hell is real, that it means being sent away from God, and that it is linked to the choices people make in life (2 marks).","evidence":["Matthew 25:41, 46","CCC 1033–1037"],"examTip":"Be accurate: the Church says hell exists, but not who is in it. Avoid saying 'God sends bad people to hell' without the free-will point.","answerTopic":"Hell"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"'A loving God would not send anyone to hell.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nChristians believe 'God is love' (1 John 4:8). A loving parent would never wish their child to suffer forever, so it seems contradictory that an all-loving God would allow eternal punishment. Jesus's parable of the Prodigal Son (Luke 15) shows a father who runs to welcome back his son, however badly he behaved.\n\nEternal punishment also seems out of proportion. Even serious sins are committed in a finite lifetime, so infinite punishment can seem unjust. For this reason some Christians are universalists: they believe all people will eventually be reconciled to God (they sometimes cite 1 Timothy 2:4, God 'desires everyone to be saved'). Others believe in annihilation, where the wicked simply cease to exist. Many non-religious people see hell as a medieval idea used to frighten people into obedience.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nCatholic teaching accepts that God is love, but it argues that hell is not God 'sending' people anywhere against their will. Hell is 'the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God' (CCC 1033). Because God created humans with free will, he respects their choice. If someone freely and finally rejects God, forcing them into heaven would be a violation of their freedom. 'God predestines no one to go to hell' (CCC 1037).\n\nJesus himself spoke of hell more than once. In Matthew 25:41, 46, those who refused to help the needy 'will go away into eternal punishment'. He also spoke of Gehenna. The Church cannot ignore this teaching. Love and justice belong together: a God who did not care about evil, such as murder or cruelty, would not be truly loving towards the victims.\n\nCatholics also note that God gives every chance to repent. The sacrament of Reconciliation, the thief on the cross (Luke 23:43) and the Divine Mercy devotion all show that mercy is always available. The Church has never declared that any particular person is in hell, and Catholics pray that all may be saved.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement has emotional force, and it is correct that God does not want anyone to be lost. However, it misunderstands Catholic teaching by picturing God actively 'sending' people to hell. The more convincing view is that hell is the consequence of a free creature's final rejection of love. A loving God who respects freedom may permit that choice, while doing everything to prevent it. So a loving God and the possibility of hell are compatible, though Catholics hope and pray that hell will be empty of human souls.","evidence":["1 John 4:8","Luke 15:11–32","Matthew 25:41, 46","CCC 1033, 1037","1 Timothy 2:4","Universalism; annihilationism"],"examTip":"Use precise Catholic wording ('self-exclusion', 'God predestines no one') to strengthen the argument against the statement.","answerTopic":"Hell – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","question":"'Belief in purgatory is the most helpful Catholic teaching about life after death.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMost people are neither perfectly holy nor completely wicked. Purgatory explains how ordinary people who die in God's friendship, but with faults and unconfessed lesser sins, can still be made ready for heaven (CCC 1030). This can be very comforting. It suggests God's mercy is patient and that a person does not have to be perfect at death to be saved.\n\nPurgatory also helps the living. Because Catholics believe in it, they can pray for those who have died, have Masses offered for them and keep All Souls' Day (2 November). This keeps a loving bond with the dead (the 'communion of saints') and gives grieving families something practical to do. The practice is rooted in 2 Maccabees 12:46, where Judas Maccabeus makes atonement for the dead 'so that they might be delivered from their sin'.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nOther teachings may be more central or helpful. The resurrection of the dead, based on Jesus's own resurrection (1 Corinthians 15), is the foundation of all Christian hope and is proclaimed in the Creed. Heaven, seeing God 'face to face' (1 Corinthians 13:12), is the goal that gives life its ultimate purpose. Purgatory is a stage on the way to heaven and only makes sense in the light of these deeper beliefs.\n\nThe teaching on judgement may be more helpful for how people live now. Matthew 25 tells Catholics that they will be judged on love for the poor, which directly motivates charity and justice. Purgatory, by contrast, has sometimes been misunderstood. In the Middle Ages abuses in the selling of indulgences contributed to the Reformation.\n\nMany Christians would not find purgatory helpful at all. Most Protestants reject it, arguing that Christ's sacrifice was fully sufficient (Hebrews 10:14), that it is not clearly taught in their Bible, and that it can make people anxious about further suffering after death. Non-religious people reject any afterlife.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nPurgatory is a distinctively Catholic belief that offers real comfort and supports practices such as prayer for the dead, so it is certainly helpful for many Catholics. However, calling it the 'most helpful' teaching is hard to defend, because it depends on the more basic beliefs in resurrection and heaven, and other Christians reject it. The more convincing view is that the resurrection, guaranteed by Christ's own rising, is the most helpful teaching, since it gives hope to all believers. Purgatory adds a valuable expression of God's mercy within that hope.","evidence":["CCC 1030–1032","2 Maccabees 12:46","1 Corinthians 13:12; 15","Matthew 25:31–46","Hebrews 10:14","All Souls' Day"],"examTip":"When a statement says 'most', compare at least two alternatives and decide between them in your conclusion.","answerTopic":"Purgatory – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Which one of the following best describes a sacrament in Catholic teaching?\nA) A prayer said privately at home\nB) An efficacious sign of grace instituted by Christ\nC) A rule made by the Pope\nD) A symbol that only reminds people of God","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) An efficacious sign of grace instituted by Christ (CCC 1131).\n\nD is wrong because Catholic sacraments do more than remind: they really give grace. A and C are not sacraments.","evidence":["CCC 1131"],"examTip":"Learn the CCC 1131 definition word-for-word. It answers many 1-mark and 6-mark questions.","answerTopic":"Sacrament definition"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"How many sacraments do most Protestant Churches, such as the Church of England, recognise?\nA) None\nB) Two\nC) Five\nD) Seven","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Two – Baptism and Holy Communion (the Eucharist), the 'dominical' sacraments commanded by Jesus himself.\n\nCatholics and Orthodox recognise seven. Quakers and the Salvation Army celebrate none outwardly.","evidence":["Book of Common Prayer, Article 25 (Church of England)","Matthew 28:19; Luke 22:19"],"examTip":"Diversity facts like these are easy marks. Know which groups recognise 7, 2 or 0 sacraments.","answerTopic":"Number of sacraments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Give one meaning of 'the sacramental nature of reality'.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• God can be encountered in and through the physical world\n• All of creation can reveal God's presence and love\n• Ordinary things, people and events can be signs of God / channels of grace\n• Because of the Incarnation, matter can carry the divine","evidence":["Romans 1:20","Laudato Si' 84"],"examTip":"A clear one-sentence definition is enough: 'God can be encountered through the physical world.'","answerTopic":"Sacramentality"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Give one example of a sacramental used by Catholics.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: holy water; a rosary; a crucifix; a blessed medal; ashes on Ash Wednesday; blessed palms; a scapular; a blessing; holy oil (outside sacraments) / a candle.","evidence":["CCC 1667–1673"],"examTip":"Don't confuse sacramentals with the seven sacraments. A rosary is a sacramental, not a sacrament.","answerTopic":"Sacramentals"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the sacramental nature of reality influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics use physical objects and actions in worship (1 mark). They bless themselves with holy water as they enter church, light candles before statues, use incense and kneel. They believe God can be met through the senses, so the material world is part of their prayer (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Catholics care for creation because they see it as revealing God (1 mark). Following Laudato Si', many parishes and schools have joined the 'Live Simply' award or run environmental projects, believing that 'the entire material universe speaks of God's love' and should be protected (2nd mark).","evidence":["Laudato Si' 84","Holy water, candles, incense"],"examTip":"Make sure your second sentence shows what Catholics do because of the belief.","answerTopic":"Sacramentality – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about sacraments.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics believe there are seven sacraments, which are efficacious signs that actually give God's grace (1 mark). For example, in Baptism the water and words truly remove original sin. This is because the sacraments work through Christ's own action (ex opere operato, CCC 1128), not just as symbols (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Quakers (the Religious Society of Friends) do not celebrate any outward sacraments (1 mark). They believe God's grace is given inwardly and directly, so no rituals or priests are needed. They see the whole of life as potentially sacramental and meet in silence to wait on the Spirit (2nd mark).\n\n(Alternatively: most Protestants recognise two sacraments, or Baptists see them as 'ordinances' or symbols of obedience.)","evidence":["CCC 1128, 1131","Quaker worship","Protestant 'two sacraments'"],"examTip":"Choose views that clearly contrast, for example seven efficacious sacraments versus none at all.","answerTopic":"Sacraments – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Explain two Catholic beliefs about the nature of sacraments.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Sacraments are visible signs that really give invisible grace (1 mark). They use matter, such as water, oil, or bread and wine, together with words. These do not just symbolise what God does but make it happen, for example truly forgiving sins in Reconciliation (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: The sacraments were instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church (1 mark). Catholics believe Jesus himself founded them during his ministry, for example by commanding the apostles to baptise (Matthew 28:19) and to celebrate the Eucharist. The Church therefore celebrates them faithfully, through ordained ministers (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: The Catechism defines the sacraments as 'efficacious signs of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is dispensed to us' (CCC 1131). This confirms both beliefs: that they actually give grace (efficacious) and that they come from Christ through the Church (2 marks).","evidence":["CCC 1131","Matthew 28:19","Luke 22:19","CCC 1128 ex opere operato"],"examTip":"The Catechism is a perfect 'source of authority' here. Give the paragraph number if you can.","answerTopic":"Nature of sacraments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"Explain two reasons why Catholics believe that God can be experienced through the physical world.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: God created the world and declared it good (1 mark). Because creation is God's work, it reflects its creator, in the way a painting reveals something of the artist. The beauty and order of nature can therefore lead people to wonder at God (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: In the Incarnation God became a physical human being (1 mark). Since God took flesh in Jesus, matter can carry the divine. That is why Catholics believe physical things such as bread and wine can become Christ's presence in the sacraments (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: St Paul writes that 'ever since the creation of the world his eternal power and divine nature… have been understood and seen through the things he has made' (Romans 1:20). This supports the belief that the physical world is a way of knowing and experiencing God (2 marks).","evidence":["Romans 1:20","Psalm 19:1","Genesis 1:31","John 1:14","Laudato Si' 84, 235"],"examTip":"Linking sacramentality to creation and the Incarnation shows you understand how Catholic beliefs connect. That impresses examiners.","answerTopic":"Sacramentality – reasons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"'Sacraments are just symbols.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nAt face value, sacraments use ordinary objects. The water in baptism is ordinary water, and the bread and wine at Mass still look, taste and feel the same after consecration. Nothing scientifically observable changes. A non-religious person might therefore argue that sacraments are powerful symbols at most, rituals that mark important stages of life and strengthen community.\n\nSome Christians agree that sacraments are symbolic. Many Baptists and evangelicals call them 'ordinances', acts of obedience that symbolise faith a person already has. The Swiss reformer Zwingli taught that the Lord's Supper is a memorial: 'Do this in remembrance of me' (Luke 22:19) means remembering, not a real change. Quakers go further and use no outward sacraments, believing grace is inward and does not need symbols at all.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic Church strongly rejects the word 'just'. The Catechism teaches that sacraments are 'efficacious signs of grace' (CCC 1131): they effect what they signify. Baptism really washes away original sin. Reconciliation really forgives sins ('If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven them', John 20:23). In the Eucharist the bread and wine really become the Body and Blood of Christ through transubstantiation (Council of Trent; CCC 1376). They are signs, but signs that work.\n\nThis is grounded in scripture. Jesus said, 'This is my body' (Luke 22:19), not 'This represents my body'. In John 6:55 he says, 'my flesh is true food'. Catholics believe that because Christ instituted the sacraments, they work ex opere operato, by Christ's power rather than human imagination (CCC 1128).\n\nSacramental thinking also flows from the Incarnation. If God could become flesh in Jesus, then God can also act through physical things. Catholics believe that reality itself is sacramental, so it is not strange that God would use water, oil, bread and wine to give his life.\n\nMany Anglicans and Lutherans also believe in a real spiritual presence of Christ in Holy Communion, so the 'just symbols' view is a minority view even among Protestants.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nSacraments certainly are symbols. They use signs that point beyond themselves, and the statement is consistent with Zwingli, Baptist and non-religious views. But for Catholics the word 'just' makes the statement false. Their faith rests on Christ's own words and the Church's teaching that sacraments actually give the grace they signify. Although this cannot be proved scientifically, the stronger argument from a Catholic perspective is that sacraments are much more than symbols: they are real encounters with Christ.","evidence":["CCC 1128, 1131, 1376","Luke 22:19","John 6:55; 20:23","Zwingli's memorialism","Baptist 'ordinances'","Quaker practice"],"examTip":"Focus on the key word ('just'). It lets you agree in part and disagree in part, which leads to a nuanced conclusion.","answerTopic":"Sacraments – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","question":"'The whole world is a sacrament.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nCatholics believe in the sacramental nature of reality. God created everything and saw that it was 'very good' (Genesis 1:31), and creation reveals his 'eternal power and divine nature' (Romans 1:20). Pope Francis writes in Laudato Si' that 'the entire material universe speaks of God's love' (LS 84) and that God can be found in 'a leaf, a mountain trail, a dewdrop'. In this broad sense the world is like a sacrament, a visible sign of the invisible God.\n\nThe Incarnation strengthens this view. God took on matter in Jesus and lived an ordinary life, working as a carpenter and eating with friends. So no part of ordinary life is outside God's reach. Christian poets such as Gerard Manley Hopkins ('The world is charged with the grandeur of God') express this sense that everything can point to God. Quakers and some other Christians, who reject outward sacraments, say that all of life is sacramental and that God can be met in every moment.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nIn strict Catholic teaching, a 'sacrament' has a precise meaning: one of seven 'efficacious signs of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church' (CCC 1131). The world as a whole was not instituted by Christ as a sacrament, and it does not give grace ex opere operato as Baptism or the Eucharist do. Calling everything a sacrament risks making the seven sacraments seem less special.\n\nThe world also contains much that does not reveal God's goodness, such as natural disasters, disease and human cruelty. Christians believe creation is wounded by sin (Romans 8:22 speaks of creation 'groaning'), so not everything is a clear sign of God. Some Christians, especially in Protestant traditions influenced by the Reformation, stress the difference between Creator and creation. They worry that seeing God in everything could slide towards pantheism (the belief that God is everything), which Christianity rejects.\n\nNon-religious people would reject the statement entirely, because the world can be explained by science without any reference to God.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is true in a broad, poetic sense, which Catholic teaching accepts as the 'sacramental nature of reality'. The world can reveal God and be a place of encounter with him. However, it is not literally true in the technical sense, because only the seven sacraments are efficacious signs instituted by Christ. The most balanced conclusion is that the world is sacramental, meaning full of signs of God, but it is not itself a sacrament. The seven sacraments remain the privileged ways in which God's grace is given.","evidence":["Genesis 1:31","Romans 1:20; 8:22","Laudato Si' 84, 233","CCC 1131","Gerard Manley Hopkins, 'God's Grandeur'","Pantheism"],"examTip":"Distinguish the technical meaning of a word from its broader meaning. This is a high-level evaluative skill.","answerTopic":"Sacramentality – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Which one of the following is a sacrament of healing?\nA) Confirmation\nB) Holy Orders\nC) Anointing of the Sick\nD) Matrimony","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) Anointing of the Sick.\n\nThe two sacraments of healing are Reconciliation and Anointing of the Sick. Confirmation is a sacrament of initiation. Holy Orders and Matrimony are sacraments at the service of communion.","evidence":["CCC 1420–1421"],"examTip":"Learn the three groups: initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist), healing (Reconciliation, Anointing), service of communion (Holy Orders, Matrimony).","answerTopic":"Groups of sacraments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"What is the Catholic term for the change of the bread and wine into the Body and Blood of Christ?\nA) Consubstantiation\nB) Transubstantiation\nC) Incarnation\nD) Memorialism","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Transubstantiation (Council of Trent; CCC 1376).\n\nThe substance changes while the appearances remain. Consubstantiation is a term sometimes (loosely) used for the Lutheran view. Memorialism is the symbolic view of Zwingli and many evangelicals. Incarnation refers to God becoming human.","evidence":["CCC 1376","Council of Trent"],"examTip":"Spell 'transubstantiation' correctly. It is a key specialist term that also helps your SPaG mark in 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Transubstantiation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Give one effect of the sacrament of Baptism.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Removes original sin (and all personal sin)\n• Makes the person a child of God / gives new life in the Spirit\n• Makes the person a member of the Church\n• Gives an indelible spiritual mark (character)\n• Opens the way to the other sacraments","evidence":["CCC 1263–1274","John 3:5"],"examTip":"Effects are what the sacrament does, not what happens in the ceremony. 'Removes original sin', not 'water is poured'.","answerTopic":"Baptism"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Give one sign (matter or action) used in the sacrament of Confirmation.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• Anointing of the forehead with chrism (holy oil)\n• Laying on of hands by the bishop\n• The words 'Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit'\n• Choosing a Confirmation name / sponsor (accept as part of the rite)","evidence":["Acts 8:14–17","CCC 1300"],"examTip":"Precise answers score: 'chrism' is better than just 'oil'.","answerTopic":"Confirmation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Real Presence of Christ in the Eucharist influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: Catholics show great reverence towards the Eucharist (1 mark). They genuflect towards the tabernacle when entering church, fast for an hour before receiving Holy Communion and receive it with an 'Amen', because they believe it truly is Jesus, not just bread (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Catholics attend Mass regularly and take part in Eucharistic adoration (1 mark). Because the Eucharist is 'the source and summit of the Christian life', they make Sunday Mass a priority. Many also spend time praying before the Blessed Sacrament in exposition or join Corpus Christi processions, as a way of being with Christ (2nd mark).","evidence":["Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324","CCC 1378–1379 (adoration)","Eucharistic fast"],"examTip":"Use specific practices (genuflection, the Eucharistic fast, adoration) to show influence clearly.","answerTopic":"Eucharist – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Explain two different Christian beliefs about the meaning of the Eucharist.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First belief: Catholics believe in transubstantiation (1 mark). At the consecration the bread and wine truly become the Body and Blood of Christ, though they still look and taste the same. Catholics take Jesus's words 'This is my body' (Luke 22:19) literally, so the consecrated host is worshipped as Christ himself (2nd mark).\n\nSecond belief: Many Protestant Christians, such as Baptists, see the Lord's Supper as a memorial (1 mark). The bread and wine remain bread and wine and are symbols that help believers remember Jesus's death, following his command 'Do this in remembrance of me'. This view comes from the reformer Zwingli (2nd mark).\n\n(Also creditworthy: the Lutheran view of real presence 'in, with and under' the bread and wine, or the Quaker view that no outward Eucharist is needed.)","evidence":["Luke 22:19","Council of Trent","Zwingli","Lutheran sacramental union"],"examTip":"Name the denomination for each view. 'Some Christians…' is vague and may cost the development mark.","answerTopic":"Eucharist – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Explain two reasons why the Eucharist is described as 'the source and summit of the Christian life'.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: It is the 'source' because the whole life of the Church flows from it (1 mark). In Holy Communion Catholics receive Christ himself, who gives them grace and strength to live as his followers during the week. The Eucharist builds up the Church as the Body of Christ, uniting Catholics with one another (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: It is the 'summit' because it is the highest point of Christian worship (1 mark). The Mass makes present Christ's one sacrifice on the cross, and all other sacraments and prayers lead towards this moment of union with God (CCC 1324), which is a foretaste of heaven (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Jesus says in John 6:54, 'Those who eat my flesh and drink my blood have eternal life, and I will raise them up on the last day.' This shows that the Eucharist is the source of eternal life, which is why the Church gives it such a central place. The phrase 'source and summit' itself comes from Lumen Gentium 11 (2 marks).","evidence":["Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324","John 6:54","1 Corinthians 10:17","CCC 1366"],"examTip":"Explain both parts of the phrase, 'source' and 'summit'. That gives you a natural two-point structure.","answerTopic":"Source and summit"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"Explain two Catholic teachings about the sacrament of Reconciliation.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: Reconciliation truly forgives sins committed after baptism (1 mark). Through the priest's words of absolution, God forgives the penitent, provided they are truly sorry (contrite), confess their sins honestly and are willing to do penance. It is required for mortal sin before receiving Communion (2nd mark).\n\nSecond teaching: Reconciliation restores a person's relationship with the Church as well as with God (1 mark). Because sin damages the whole community, the priest acts in the name of the Church, and the penitent is reconciled with others. Penance, such as prayer or putting right a wrong, helps repair the harm done (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: After the resurrection Jesus breathed on the apostles and said, 'Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven them' (John 20:22–23). Catholics believe this gave the apostles, and the priests who succeed them, the authority to forgive sins in Christ's name (2 marks).","evidence":["John 20:22–23","Luke 15:11–32 (Prodigal Son)","CCC 1422–1424, 1440–1449"],"examTip":"Learn the four parts of the rite: contrition, confession, penance, absolution. They make excellent development.","answerTopic":"Reconciliation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"'The Eucharist is the most important sacrament.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Catholic Church calls the Eucharist 'the source and summit of the Christian life' (Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324). No other sacrament is described in this way. The Catechism says that the other sacraments 'are bound up with the Eucharist and are oriented toward it'. In the Eucharist, Catholics believe they receive not just grace but Christ himself, really present through transubstantiation. 'My flesh is true food and my blood is true drink' (John 6:55).\n\nThe Eucharist is also the sacrament Catholics receive most often, weekly or even daily, so it has the greatest ongoing impact on their spiritual life. It makes present Christ's sacrifice on the cross (CCC 1366), unites the Church as one body (1 Corinthians 10:17) and gives 'eternal life' (John 6:54). It is why Catholics must attend Mass on Sundays and why churches have a tabernacle and sanctuary lamp.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nBaptism could be seen as more important, because without it a person cannot receive any other sacrament. It is 'the gateway to life in the Spirit' (CCC 1213). It removes original sin and makes someone a member of the Church, and Jesus said 'no one can enter the kingdom of God without being born of water and Spirit' (John 3:5). In an emergency, anyone can baptise, which shows how vital the Church considers it.\n\nOthers might argue that Reconciliation matters most for someone who has committed serious sin, because it restores the life of grace, and a person in mortal sin should not receive Communion until they have been reconciled. For a dying person, the Anointing of the Sick, together with Viaticum (final Communion), may feel most important.\n\nMany Protestants would disagree with the Catholic emphasis on the Eucharist altogether. For memorialists, the Lord's Supper is a symbolic act of remembrance, important but not more important than preaching the Word. Quakers do not practise it at all.\n\nIt can also be argued that ranking sacraments is unhelpful. They all come from Christ and together accompany a person through life, from birth (Baptism), through growing up (Confirmation) and calling (Matrimony or Holy Orders), to sickness and death (Anointing).\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nBaptism is necessary first, and each sacrament has its own role. However, the strongest argument from a Catholic perspective is that the Eucharist is the most important, because the Church explicitly teaches that it is the source and summit of Christian life and that the other sacraments are directed towards it. Baptism opens the door, but the Eucharist is the goal of Christian initiation and the repeated meeting with Christ himself. So the statement is justified within Catholic teaching, even if other Christians would not agree.","evidence":["Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324, 1213, 1366","John 6:54–55; 3:5","1 Corinthians 10:17","Memorialist and Quaker views"],"examTip":"Weigh the Eucharist against at least one other named sacrament, such as Baptism. A comparison gives real evaluation.","answerTopic":"Eucharist – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","question":"'Infant baptism should not be allowed because babies cannot choose for themselves.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nBaptism involves making promises to reject sin and believe in God. A baby cannot understand or make those promises, so it can be argued that the sacrament is being done to them rather than chosen by them. Many people today value personal choice and believe religion should be freely chosen when a person is old enough to understand.\n\nSome Christians agree for religious reasons. Baptists and many Pentecostal churches practise 'believer's baptism', usually by full immersion, for those old enough to profess faith. They point out that in the New Testament people were baptised after repenting and believing: 'Repent, and be baptised every one of you' (Acts 2:38). Jesus himself was baptised as an adult (Matthew 3). On this view, infant baptism has no clear biblical basis.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic Church teaches that baptism is a free gift of God's grace, not a reward for a choice. Because children are born with original sin, baptism frees them from it and makes them children of God (CCC 1250). Waiting would deny them this grace. The Church has baptised infants since early times, and the New Testament records whole 'households' being baptised (Acts 16:15, 33), which probably included children.\n\nParents make many important choices for their children, such as diet, education and language, because they love them and want the best for them. Catholic parents believe faith is the greatest gift they can give. At the baptism the parents and godparents make the promises on the child's behalf and commit to bringing them up in the faith. The child later confirms this personally at Confirmation, so they do have their own choice later.\n\nJesus welcomed children: 'Let the little children come to me… for it is to such as these that the kingdom of God belongs' (Mark 10:14). Catholics see infant baptism as following this welcome. Grace comes before any human response, just as God's love comes before we can love him.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement raises a fair point about personal choice, and it is supported by Baptist practice and by many non-religious people. However, it assumes baptism is mainly about the person's own decision. For Catholics, baptism is mainly about God's gift: removing original sin and welcoming a child into God's family. The child is later free to affirm or reject that faith. Given this, the more convincing argument, from a Catholic perspective, is that infant baptism should be allowed, because it gives children grace from the start and does not remove their later freedom.","evidence":["CCC 1250–1252","Acts 2:38; 16:15, 33","Mark 10:14","Matthew 3","Believer's baptism (Baptists)"],"examTip":"Bring in different Christian views (Catholic versus Baptist) as well as non-religious views. That shows a 'reasoned consideration of different points of view'.","answerTopic":"Baptism – evaluation"}],"paper2":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Which one of the following is the Catholic definition of prayer quoted in the Catechism?\nA) 'The source and summit of the Christian life'\nB) 'The raising of one's mind and heart to God'\nC) 'An outward sign of inward grace'\nD) 'Love your neighbour as yourself'","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) 'The raising of one's mind and heart to God' (CCC 2559, quoting St John Damascene).\n\nA describes the Eucharist, C describes a sacrament, and D is Jesus's commandment (Mark 12:31).","evidence":["CCC 2559"],"examTip":"Several AQA phrases are quoted in the specification. Learn them exactly, because they make ideal MCQ material.","answerTopic":"Definition of prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"In which Gospel passage do the disciples ask Jesus 'Lord, teach us to pray' before he teaches them the Lord's Prayer?\nA) Mark 16\nB) John 3\nC) Luke 11\nD) Acts 2","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) Luke 11 (verses 1–4).\n\nMatthew 6:9–13 gives the longer version within the Sermon on the Mount. Mark 16 is the resurrection, John 3 is Jesus and Nicodemus, and Acts 2 is Pentecost.","evidence":["Luke 11:1–4","Matthew 6:9–13"],"examTip":"Know both Gospel locations of the Lord's Prayer: Matthew 6 and Luke 11.","answerTopic":"Lord's Prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Give one example of a formal (set) prayer used by Catholics.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: the Our Father (Lord's Prayer); the Hail Mary; the Glory Be; the Apostles' or Nicene Creed; the Angelus; the Rosary; the Liturgy of the Hours / Divine Office; grace before meals; the Act of Contrition; the Mass.","evidence":["Matthew 6:9–13","Luke 1:28, 42 (Hail Mary)"],"examTip":"Give one named prayer. Don't explain it in a 1-mark answer.","answerTopic":"Formal prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Give one reason why some Catholics prefer informal prayer.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from:\n• It is personal and can express exactly what the person feels\n• It can be said anywhere at any time\n• It feels like talking to God as a friend / a closer relationship\n• It avoids words becoming mechanical or repetitive (Matthew 6:7)\n• Jesus prayed alone in his own words (e.g. Gethsemane)","evidence":["Matthew 6:6–7"],"examTip":"One reason is enough. 'It is personal' is acceptable.","answerTopic":"Informal prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Explain two ways in which the Lord's Prayer influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: The Lord's Prayer is a model for how Catholics pray (1 mark). It begins by praising God ('hallowed be thy name') before asking for needs, so Catholics learn to put God first in their own prayers. They pray it daily and at every Mass (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: It encourages Catholics to forgive others (1 mark). In praying 'forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us', they recognise that receiving God's forgiveness depends on forgiving others. This may lead them to seek reconciliation in their families or communities, even after serious harm (2nd mark).","evidence":["Matthew 6:9–13","Matthew 6:14–15","CCC 2838–2845"],"examTip":"Pick two different petitions or features of the prayer. Each gives a ready-made 'way'.","answerTopic":"Lord's Prayer – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Explain two different Christian views about the importance of formal and informal prayer.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Many Catholics and Orthodox Christians value formal prayer highly (1 mark). Set prayers like the Our Father, the Rosary and the Liturgy of the Hours unite the whole Church in the same words and express the faith accurately. The Mass, a formal liturgy, is the highest form of prayer (2nd mark).\n\nSecond view: Many evangelical and Pentecostal Christians prefer informal, spontaneous prayer (1 mark). They believe prayer should come from the heart in a person's own words, led by the Holy Spirit, and worry that repeating set words can become 'empty phrases' (Matthew 6:7), so their worship often includes extempore prayer (2nd mark).","evidence":["Matthew 6:7","Liturgy of the Hours","Pentecostal/charismatic prayer"],"examTip":"To show the views are different, connect each to a named tradition and give a reason for it.","answerTopic":"Formal vs informal – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Explain two reasons why prayer is important for Catholics.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: Prayer builds a personal relationship with God (1 mark). Just as friendships need communication, Catholics believe they grow closer to God by raising their 'mind and heart' to him (CCC 2559), listening as well as speaking, which strengthens their faith (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: Prayer gives strength and guidance in difficult times (1 mark). Catholics bring their worries, illnesses and decisions to God and pray for others (intercession), trusting that God hears and responds, even if not always in the way they expect (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Jesus himself prayed often, for example in Gethsemane: 'not my will but yours be done' (Luke 22:42). This shows Catholics that prayer is important because Jesus did it, and that it helps a person accept God's will even in suffering (2 marks).","evidence":["CCC 2559","Luke 22:42","1 Thessalonians 5:17","Matthew 7:7 'Ask, and it will be given you'"],"examTip":"Jesus's own example of prayer is an excellent source of authority for 'why is prayer important?' questions.","answerTopic":"Importance of prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"Explain two things the Lord's Prayer teaches Catholics about God.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First teaching: God is a loving Father (1 mark). Jesus teaches his followers to call God 'Father' (Abba), which shows a close, trusting relationship like that of a child with a good parent, and 'Our' shows that all believers are brothers and sisters in one family (2nd mark).\n\nSecond teaching: God is holy and sovereign (1 mark). 'Hallowed be thy name' and 'thy kingdom come, thy will be done' show that God deserves reverence and that his will should rule over the earth. Catholics should therefore work to bring about God's kingdom of justice and peace (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: The Lord's Prayer is found in Matthew 6:9–13, where Jesus says 'Pray then in this way: Our Father in heaven…'. Because Jesus himself gave these words, Catholics treat the prayer as the perfect model of prayer, which Tertullian called 'the summary of the whole gospel' (CCC 2761) (2 marks).","evidence":["Matthew 6:9–13","Luke 11:2–4","CCC 2761","Romans 8:15 'Abba! Father!'"],"examTip":"Quote phrases from the prayer itself as evidence. Each phrase can unlock a belief.","answerTopic":"Lord's Prayer – meaning"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"'Informal prayer is more valuable than formal prayer.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nInformal prayer comes straight from the heart. A person can speak to God honestly about their own situation, such as an exam, an illness or a broken friendship, in their own words. This makes prayer a real conversation and relationship, which fits the Catholic definition of prayer as 'the raising of one's mind and heart to God' (CCC 2559). Jesus warned against heaping up 'empty phrases' (Matthew 6:7) and taught people to pray 'in secret' (Matthew 6:6).\n\nJesus himself often prayed informally. In Gethsemane he prayed, in his own words, 'remove this cup from me; yet, not my will but yours be done' (Luke 22:42). Many evangelical and Pentecostal Christians, and Catholic charismatic groups, believe the Holy Spirit inspires spontaneous prayer and that it can be more alive than reading set words.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nFormal prayer has great value. Set prayers such as the Our Father were given by Jesus himself: 'Pray then in this way' (Matthew 6:9). If Jesus gave his followers a fixed prayer, it cannot be less valuable than our own words. The Church's greatest prayer, the Mass, is formal. Vatican II called the liturgy 'the summit toward which the activity of the Church is directed' (Sacrosanctum Concilium 10).\n\nFormal prayer unites Catholics across the world and across history. Saying the same Creed or Hail Mary links a believer to millions of others and to the saints. It expresses the faith accurately ('the law of prayer is the law of belief'). It also helps when people are too upset or tired to find words, for example praying the Rosary at a hospital bedside. It can be a form of meditation, as the repeated words free the mind to reflect on the mysteries of Christ.\n\nInformal prayer has weaknesses too. It may become self-centred, focused only on asking for things, or theologically confused. Formal prayers teach a balanced pattern of adoration, contrition, thanksgiving and petition.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nInformal prayer can be deeply personal and sincere, and the statement rightly warns against formal prayer becoming mechanical. However, formal prayer is also sincere when said with attention, and it carries the authority of Jesus and the Church. The most convincing view, and the Catholic one, is that neither is more valuable. Each feeds the other: personal prayer helps someone mean the words of the liturgy, and formal prayer shapes and steadies personal prayer. What matters most is the heart of the person praying.","evidence":["CCC 2559","Matthew 6:6–9","Luke 22:42","Sacrosanctum Concilium 10","Lex orandi, lex credendi"],"examTip":"A 'neither is better – they work together' conclusion is fine, but only if you have argued strongly for both sides first.","answerTopic":"Formal vs informal – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","question":"'The Lord's Prayer is the only prayer a Catholic needs.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Lord's Prayer was taught by Jesus himself when his disciples asked, 'Lord, teach us to pray' (Luke 11:1). No other prayer has such direct authority. Tertullian called it 'the summary of the whole gospel' (CCC 2761), because it contains everything essential to Christian faith: praise of God, longing for his kingdom, obedience to his will, trust for daily needs, forgiveness and protection from evil.\n\nIt covers every type of prayer. 'Hallowed be thy name' is adoration, the petitions for bread and forgiveness are supplication and contrition, and the doxology, used by many Christians, is praise. Because it includes everything, some might argue a Catholic needs nothing more. It is also prayed by Christians of almost every denomination, so it unites the whole Church.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic tradition contains a rich variety of prayer. The Mass, the 'source and summit' of Christian life, goes far beyond the Lord's Prayer, though the Our Father is part of it. Catholics also pray the Hail Mary and the Rosary, asking for the intercession of Mary, and they pray to the saints. The Liturgy of the Hours draws on the Psalms. The Church encourages meditation and contemplative prayer (CCC 2705–2719), which may involve silence rather than words at all.\n\nJesus himself prayed in other ways. He prayed the Psalms ('My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?', Mark 15:34, quoting Psalm 22) and prayed in his own words in Gethsemane. St Paul told Christians to 'pray without ceasing' (1 Thessalonians 5:17), which suggests prayer should fill the whole of life in many forms, not just one set text.\n\nPeople's needs also differ. A grieving person, someone giving thanks for a new baby or a young person praying about a decision may need to speak personally to God. Informal prayer allows this.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe Lord's Prayer is the perfect model of prayer and is central to Catholic life. In that sense the statement points to something true: if someone could pray only one prayer, this would be the best choice. However, the word 'only' does not fit Catholic teaching or practice, which values the Mass, Marian prayer, the Psalms, meditation and personal prayer. The stronger conclusion is that the Lord's Prayer is the pattern for all prayer, not a replacement for it.","evidence":["Luke 11:1–4","Matthew 6:9–13","CCC 2761, 2705–2719","Mark 15:34 (Psalm 22)","1 Thessalonians 5:17"],"examTip":"Test the key word ('only') against real examples of Catholic prayer: the Mass, the Rosary, the Psalms and contemplation.","answerTopic":"Lord's Prayer – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"How many Hail Marys are prayed in one decade of the Rosary?\nA) Five\nB) Seven\nC) Ten\nD) Fourteen","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) Ten.\n\nEach decade has one Our Father, ten Hail Marys and a Glory Be. Five is the number of mysteries in each set, and fourteen is the number of traditional Stations of the Cross.","evidence":["Structure of the Rosary"],"examTip":"Don't confuse the numbers: 5 mysteries per set, 10 Hail Marys per decade, 14 Stations.","answerTopic":"Rosary"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Which set of mysteries of the Rosary was added by Pope John Paul II in 2002?\nA) Joyful\nB) Sorrowful\nC) Glorious\nD) Luminous","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: D) Luminous (the 'Mysteries of Light'), added in Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002).\n\nThey cover Jesus's public ministry, from his Baptism to the Institution of the Eucharist.","evidence":["Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002)"],"examTip":"Learn the four sets and one or two examples from each.","answerTopic":"Mysteries of the Rosary"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Give one event remembered in the Stations of the Cross.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: Jesus is condemned to death; Jesus takes up his cross; Jesus falls (first/second/third time); Jesus meets his mother; Simon of Cyrene helps carry the cross; Veronica wipes Jesus's face; Jesus meets the women of Jerusalem; Jesus is stripped; Jesus is nailed to the cross; Jesus dies; Jesus is taken down from the cross; Jesus is laid in the tomb.","evidence":["Mark 15:21","Luke 23:26–28"],"examTip":"Any single station is fine. Simon of Cyrene is a safe, Gospel-based choice.","answerTopic":"Stations of the Cross"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Give one time of year when Catholics especially pray the Stations of the Cross.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: Lent (especially the Fridays of Lent); Good Friday; Holy Week.","evidence":["Liturgical year"],"examTip":"A single time is enough: 'Good Friday'.","answerTopic":"Stations – when"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Explain two ways in which praying the Rosary influences Catholics today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: The Rosary helps Catholics reflect on the life of Christ (1 mark). As they pray each decade they meditate on a mystery, for example the Crucifixion or the Resurrection. This deepens their understanding of the Gospel and their love for Jesus. John Paul II called it a 'compendium of the Gospel' (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: The Rosary brings Catholics together in prayer and gives comfort in hard times (1 mark). Families or parish groups pray it together, especially in October and at vigils before funerals. Many Catholics carry rosary beads and pray when they are anxious or ill, finding peace in the repeated prayers and in asking for Mary's intercession (2nd mark).","evidence":["Rosarium Virginis Mariae","Luke 1:28, 42"],"examTip":"Use practical details (October, funeral vigils, prayer groups, beads) to make the influence concrete.","answerTopic":"Rosary – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Explain two different Christian views about the Rosary.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Catholics see the Rosary as a powerful, Christ-centred prayer (1 mark). Through Mary, they meditate on the mysteries of Jesus's life. They believe Mary intercedes for them, and the Church has strongly encouraged it, for example in John Paul II's Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond view: Many Protestant Christians do not use the Rosary (1 mark). They believe prayer should be addressed directly to God through Christ, the 'one mediator' (1 Timothy 2:5). They are uneasy about prayers asking for Mary's intercession and think repetitive prayer can become the 'empty phrases' Jesus warned against (Matthew 6:7) (2nd mark).","evidence":["1 Timothy 2:5","Matthew 6:7","Rosarium Virginis Mariae"],"examTip":"Give the reason behind each view, ideally with a Bible verse. That earns the development mark.","answerTopic":"Rosary – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Explain two reasons why the Stations of the Cross are important to Catholics.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: The Stations help Catholics remember and reflect on Jesus's suffering and death for their salvation (1 mark). By stopping at each scene they think about what Jesus endured out of love, which leads to sorrow for sin and gratitude for redemption, especially in Lent and on Good Friday (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: The Stations act as a spiritual pilgrimage (1 mark). They began with pilgrims walking the Via Dolorosa in Jerusalem. By moving around the church, Catholics who cannot travel to the Holy Land can still 'walk with Jesus' and unite their own suffering with his (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Jesus says, 'If any want to become my followers, let them deny themselves and take up their cross daily and follow me' (Luke 9:23). The Stations put this into practice: Catholics literally follow Jesus's path to the cross and are inspired to bear their own crosses with faith (2 marks).","evidence":["Luke 9:23","Mark 15:21","Luke 23:27–28","CCC 1674"],"examTip":"Link the practice to a belief, here redemption and discipleship, to show understanding rather than just description.","answerTopic":"Stations – importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"Explain two reasons why Catholics pray the Rosary.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: To meditate on the life of Jesus (1 mark). Each decade focuses on a mystery, such as the Nativity or the Resurrection, so the Rosary is a way of reflecting on the Gospel story and growing closer to Christ (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: To ask for Mary's prayers and to honour her (1 mark). Catholics believe Mary, as Mother of God and of the Church, intercedes for them. In each Hail Mary they ask her to 'pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death' (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: The first part of the Hail Mary comes directly from Luke's Gospel. The angel Gabriel says, 'Greetings, favoured one! The Lord is with you' (Luke 1:28), and Elizabeth says, 'Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb' (Luke 1:42). This shows Catholics that honouring Mary is rooted in scripture (2 marks).","evidence":["Luke 1:28, 42","Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002)","CCC 2678, 2708"],"examTip":"Show that the Hail Mary is biblical. This is a good way to apply a source of authority.","answerTopic":"Rosary – reasons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"'Popular piety, such as the Rosary, is less important than the Mass.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Catholic Church teaches that the Eucharist is 'the source and summit of the Christian life' (Lumen Gentium 11). At Mass, Catholics believe Christ's sacrifice on the cross is made present and that they receive Christ himself in Holy Communion. No devotion can offer this. Attending Mass on Sundays is an obligation, whereas praying the Rosary or the Stations is optional.\n\nThe Catechism states that popular devotions 'extend the liturgical life of the Church, but do not replace it' (CCC 1675). The Directory on Popular Piety and the Liturgy (2001) warns against devotions becoming more important to people than the liturgy. So, according to the Church's own teaching, popular piety is secondary to the Mass.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nPopular piety can be just as important in a Catholic's daily life. Most people cannot attend Mass every day, but they can pray a decade of the Rosary on the bus or at home. John Paul II called the Rosary a 'compendium of the Gospel'. Through its mysteries it leads people to the same events, the Incarnation, death and resurrection of Jesus, that the Mass celebrates. In this way devotions help people prepare for Mass and live it out.\n\nIn some situations popular devotion may be the main way a person prays. In countries where priests are scarce, communities may gather for the Rosary when no Mass is possible. During the COVID-19 lockdowns, when churches were closed, many Catholics turned to the Rosary and the Stations at home. Popular piety also expresses the faith of a culture, for example through processions or Good Friday Stations through city streets, and can reach people who rarely attend Mass.\n\nMany Protestants would see neither the Rosary nor the Mass as essential in the Catholic sense. Some would value private Bible reading and spontaneous prayer above both, which shows that the importance of each depends on one's tradition.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement agrees with official Catholic teaching: the Mass is the highest form of worship and devotions should never replace it. However, 'less important' should not mean 'unimportant'. Popular piety feeds the faith of ordinary Catholics every day and leads them back to the liturgy. The most convincing conclusion is that the Mass is more important in itself, because only it makes Christ's sacrifice present, but popular piety is a valuable and necessary support for the Mass, not a rival to it.","evidence":["Lumen Gentium 11","CCC 1674–1675","Directory on Popular Piety and the Liturgy (2001)","Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002)"],"examTip":"Use the Church's own teaching (CCC 1675) to judge the statement. It shows strong knowledge applied to the issue.","answerTopic":"Popular piety – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","question":"'The Stations of the Cross are the best way for Catholics to prepare for Easter.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nEaster celebrates Christ's victory over death, which cannot be understood without the cross. The Stations of the Cross lead Catholics step by step through Jesus's passion, from his condemnation to his burial. This helps them appreciate the cost of their redemption. Praying them on the Fridays of Lent builds up to Good Friday, when the Pope leads the Stations at the Colosseum and parishes gather to walk the Way of the Cross.\n\nThe Stations are active and accessible. People of all ages can take part, moving around the church, kneeling and reflecting on pictures. They put into practice Jesus's call to 'take up their cross daily and follow me' (Luke 9:23), so they shape how Catholics respond to their own suffering. Modern versions link stations to present-day suffering, such as refugees, so they can inspire compassion and action.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Church teaches that the liturgy is central and that devotions 'do not replace it' (CCC 1675). The best preparation for Easter is arguably Lent as a whole: prayer, fasting and almsgiving (Matthew 6:1–18), beginning on Ash Wednesday. The sacrament of Reconciliation is especially encouraged before Easter so that Catholics can celebrate it in a state of grace. The Easter Triduum liturgies themselves, the Mass of the Lord's Supper, the Good Friday Celebration of the Passion and the Easter Vigil, are the high point of the Church's year.\n\nThe Stations also focus only on suffering and end at the tomb, unless a 15th station is added. Preparation for Easter should also include hope and joy. Some stations, such as Veronica and the three falls, come from tradition rather than scripture. That is why John Paul II introduced a Scriptural Way of the Cross in 1991.\n\nAlmsgiving is also important. Catholics give to CAFOD's Lent appeal or a Trócaire box, showing that preparation for Easter involves loving one's neighbour, not only personal devotion.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe Stations are a powerful and much-loved way of preparing for Easter, because they place the cross at the centre and invite personal response. However, calling them the 'best' way overlooks the Church's teaching that the sacraments and liturgy come first, and that Lent involves prayer, fasting and almsgiving together. The stronger conclusion is that the Stations are one valuable part of Lenten preparation, especially on Good Friday, but Reconciliation, the Triduum and acts of charity are at least as important.","evidence":["Luke 9:23","Matthew 6:1–18","CCC 1675","Easter Triduum","Scriptural Way of the Cross (1991)"],"examTip":"Offer realistic alternatives (Reconciliation, the Triduum, almsgiving). Comparison is the heart of evaluation.","answerTopic":"Stations – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Who reported seeing apparitions of Mary at Lourdes in 1858?\nA) St Teresa of Calcutta\nB) Bernadette Soubirous\nC) Lady Richeldis de Faverches\nD) St Francis of Assisi","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: B) Bernadette Soubirous.\n\nShe reported 18 apparitions at the Grotto of Massabielle in 1858. Richeldis is linked to Walsingham (1061). Mother Teresa and Francis of Assisi are unconnected to Lourdes.","evidence":["Lourdes, 1858"],"examTip":"Know the key facts of your chosen pilgrimage site: who, when, what happened and what pilgrims do.","answerTopic":"Lourdes"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Which place of Catholic pilgrimage in England is known as 'England's Nazareth'?\nA) Canterbury\nB) Lindisfarne\nC) Walsingham\nD) Iona","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Answer: C) Walsingham (Norfolk).\n\nAccording to tradition, in 1061 Lady Richeldis had a vision and built a replica of the Holy House of Nazareth there. Iona is in Scotland and is associated with an ecumenical community. Canterbury and Lindisfarne are linked to St Thomas Becket and St Cuthbert.","evidence":["Walsingham, 1061"],"examTip":"Iona is Scotland, not England. Watch for geographical distractors.","answerTopic":"Walsingham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Give one reason why a Catholic might go on pilgrimage.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: to grow closer to God; to pray in a holy place; to seek healing; to ask forgiveness / do penance; to give thanks; to honour Mary or a saint; to strengthen faith; to meet other believers; to reflect away from daily life; to help the sick (as a volunteer).","evidence":["CCC 2691"],"examTip":"Only your first reason is marked. Pick the clearest one.","answerTopic":"Purposes of pilgrimage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Give one activity that pilgrims take part in at Lourdes.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: praying at the Grotto; bathing in or drinking the spring water; attending Mass; the torchlight (Marian) procession; the Eucharistic procession / blessing of the sick; going to Confession; lighting candles; volunteering to help sick pilgrims.","evidence":["Grotto of Massabielle","Torchlight procession"],"examTip":"Make it specific to the site. 'Torchlight procession' is better than 'praying'.","answerTopic":"Lourdes – activities"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Explain two ways in which going on pilgrimage might influence a Catholic.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First way: A pilgrimage can strengthen a Catholic's faith (1 mark). Praying at a holy place like the Grotto at Lourdes, alongside thousands of others in the torchlight procession, can make them feel closer to God and part of the worldwide Church. Many return home with a renewed commitment to prayer and Mass (2nd mark).\n\nSecond way: Pilgrimage can lead a Catholic to serve others (1 mark). Many young people go to Lourdes as volunteers with diocesan pilgrimages or HCPT, caring for sick and disabled pilgrims. This experience can inspire them to keep helping vulnerable people at home, for example by joining the SVP (2nd mark).","evidence":["CCC 2691","HCPT","Diocesan Lourdes pilgrimages"],"examTip":"Influence questions can be about inner change (faith) as well as outer actions (service). Use one of each.","answerTopic":"Pilgrimage – influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Explain two different Christian views about pilgrimage.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First view: Catholics believe pilgrimage is a valuable act of worship and devotion (1 mark). Holy places like Lourdes, Rome and Jerusalem help believers pray, seek healing and grow in faith. The Catechism says pilgrimages 'evoke our earthly journey toward heaven' (CCC 2691) (2nd mark).\n\nSecond view: Many Protestant Christians believe pilgrimage is unnecessary (1 mark). They argue that God is present everywhere and can be worshipped 'in spirit and truth' anywhere (John 4:23). Some worry pilgrimage can encourage superstition about holy water or relics, or the idea of earning God's favour, and that the money could be better spent on the poor (2nd mark).","evidence":["CCC 2691","John 4:21–24","Reformation critique of pilgrimage"],"examTip":"Give the reason for each view. A Bible verse makes the Protestant view much stronger.","answerTopic":"Pilgrimage – diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Explain two reasons why Lourdes is an important place of pilgrimage for Catholics.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First reason: Catholics believe Mary appeared there to Bernadette Soubirous in 1858 (1 mark). The Lady called herself 'the Immaculate Conception' and asked for prayer and penance. The Church recognised the apparitions in 1862, so pilgrims go to honour Mary and respond to her call to conversion (2nd mark).\n\nSecond reason: Lourdes is associated with healing (1 mark). Pilgrims, especially the sick, bathe in or drink the spring water that began to flow when Bernadette dug at the Lady's request. About 70 cures have been officially recognised as miracles, and many more people report spiritual healing such as peace and strength to cope with illness (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: The Catechism teaches that 'pilgrimages evoke our earthly journey toward heaven and are traditionally very special occasions for renewal in prayer' (CCC 2691). Lourdes is important because it offers this renewal. At the Grotto, in the processions and at Mass, pilgrims renew their prayer and remember that life is a journey towards God (2 marks).","evidence":["CCC 2691","Lourdes apparitions 1858","James 5:14–15 (prayer for the sick)","Lourdes Medical Bureau"],"examTip":"If you study a different site (e.g. Walsingham, Rome), the same structure works: history, plus what it offers pilgrims, plus a source.","answerTopic":"Lourdes – importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"Explain two purposes of Catholic pilgrimage.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"First purpose: To grow closer to God through prayer (1 mark). Away from the distractions of everyday life, pilgrims spend time at Mass, in prayer and in quiet reflection at a holy place, which can deepen their relationship with God (2nd mark).\n\nSecond purpose: To seek forgiveness and spiritual healing (1 mark). A pilgrimage can be an act of penance. The effort of the journey and the chance to receive the sacrament of Reconciliation help people to turn away from sin and make a fresh start. Some also seek physical healing (2nd mark).\n\nSource of authority: Psalm 122 begins, 'I was glad when they said to me, \"Let us go to the house of the LORD!\"' It was a song of Jewish pilgrims travelling to Jerusalem, and Jesus himself went to Jerusalem for Passover (Luke 2:41). This shows that journeying to a holy place to worship God is rooted in scripture and in the example of Jesus (2 marks).","evidence":["Psalm 122:1","Luke 2:41–42","Hebrews 13:14","CCC 2691"],"examTip":"'Purpose' means why, so start each point with 'to…'.","answerTopic":"Purposes of pilgrimage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"'Pilgrimage is a waste of time and money.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nChristians believe God is everywhere. Jesus told the Samaritan woman that true worshippers would worship 'in spirit and truth', not only on a particular mountain or in Jerusalem (John 4:21–24). If God can be met in a local church, at home or in nature, travelling hundreds of miles to a shrine seems unnecessary. Many Protestant Christians, following the Reformers, hold this view.\n\nPilgrimage can be expensive. A trip to Lourdes, Rome or the Holy Land can cost hundreds or thousands of pounds, which could instead be given to the poor. Jesus taught that serving 'the least of these' is serving him (Matthew 25:40). Some sites have become commercialised, with souvenir shops and crowds, and critics argue that some practices, such as collecting holy water, can become superstitious. A humanist may also see claims of miraculous healing as unproven.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic Church teaches that pilgrimages 'evoke our earthly journey toward heaven and are traditionally very special occasions for renewal in prayer' (CCC 2691). Being somewhere holy, where saints have prayed or where Mary is believed to have appeared, can help people feel God's presence powerfully and change their lives. Many pilgrims return with deeper faith, peace or healing of fear and grief.\n\nPilgrimage has deep roots in scripture. Jewish pilgrims sang Psalm 122, 'Let us go to the house of the LORD', and Jesus travelled to Jerusalem for Passover (Luke 2:41). The Church calls itself a 'pilgrim people', so pilgrimage is a living image of the Christian life.\n\nPilgrimage often includes service. At Lourdes, thousands of volunteers, many of them young people, care for sick and disabled pilgrims, and groups like HCPT give children holidays they would never otherwise have. The experience of community, sacrifice and care for others can last a lifetime. It is not wasted. Many pilgrimages are also affordable or local, such as walking to Walsingham or a diocesan shrine.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is right that God is present everywhere and that money spent on travel could help the poor. A pilgrimage made only for tourism or superstition would be hard to justify. However, for Catholics, a pilgrimage can bring real spiritual renewal, express faith publicly and involve practical love for the sick. It also has a basis in scripture and Church teaching. The more convincing view is that pilgrimage is not a waste when it is made with the right intentions, though it is not essential, and Catholics should balance it with their duty to the poor.","evidence":["CCC 2691","John 4:21–24","Matthew 25:40","Psalm 122:1","Luke 2:41","Lumen Gentium 48 ('pilgrim Church')","HCPT"],"examTip":"Use concrete examples from your chosen site (Lourdes processions, volunteers, cures). They turn general points into strong evidence.","answerTopic":"Pilgrimage – evaluation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","question":"'Pilgrimage is the best way for a Catholic to grow closer to God.'\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• refer to Catholic teaching\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nA pilgrimage takes a person out of ordinary routines and devotes days or weeks to God. At a site such as Lourdes, pilgrims attend Mass daily, go to Confession, pray at the Grotto and join thousands of others in processions. This concentrated experience can be more intense than anything at home. Many people describe a pilgrimage as a turning point in their faith.\n\nHoly places can make faith feel real. Walking where Jesus walked in Jerusalem, or praying at St Peter's tomb in Rome, links pilgrims with the history of their faith. The Catechism describes pilgrimages as 'very special occasions for renewal in prayer' (CCC 2691). The sacrifice involved, such as walking the Camino to Santiago, can deepen humility and trust in God.\n\nARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\nThe Catholic Church teaches that the Eucharist, not pilgrimage, is 'the source and summit of the Christian life' (Lumen Gentium 11). Catholics can receive Christ himself at any Sunday Mass in their local parish. Pilgrimage is a form of popular piety, which the Church says supports the liturgy but 'does not replace it' (CCC 1675). On this view, the best way to grow closer to God is regular Mass, prayer and the sacraments.\n\nDaily prayer is available to everyone, every day, whereas most people go on pilgrimage rarely or never. Jesus taught his followers to pray to the Father 'in secret' (Matthew 6:6), and St Paul says 'pray without ceasing' (1 Thessalonians 5:17). Growing closer to God is a lifelong process, not a one-off trip.\n\nLoving one's neighbour is also a way to meet God. Jesus says 'just as you did it to one of the least of these… you did it to me' (Matthew 25:40). Volunteering with the SVP or a food bank may bring a person closer to Christ than travelling to a shrine. Many Protestants would add that God is equally present everywhere (John 4:23), so special places are not needed. Some people with disabilities or little money cannot travel at all, so pilgrimage cannot be the 'best' way for everyone.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nPilgrimage can be a powerful, life-changing way to grow closer to God, especially as a time of renewal. But Catholic teaching places the Eucharist and the sacraments at the centre of the spiritual life, together with daily prayer and love of neighbour. These are available to all, all the time. The more convincing conclusion is that pilgrimage is a valuable extra that can refresh faith, but it is not the best way. Regular Mass and prayer, lived out in service, matter more.","evidence":["CCC 2691, 1675","Lumen Gentium 11","Matthew 6:6; 25:40","1 Thessalonians 5:17","John 4:23"],"examTip":"Compare pilgrimage with everyday practices (Mass, prayer, service). Weighing alternatives is what earns Level 4.","answerTopic":"Pilgrimage – evaluation"}]},"notes":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","title":"The Trinity: one God in three persons, as expressed in the Nicene Creed","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, key beliefs: the Trinity (one God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit) as expressed in the Nicene Creed; its scriptural basis and its development at the Council of Nicaea.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: Catholics believe in one God who exists eternally as three distinct, co-equal persons: God the Father, God the Son and God the Holy Spirit. This is the Trinity. The Catechism calls it \"the central mystery of Christian faith and life\" (CCC 234). It is a mystery: it can be believed and partly understood, but never fully grasped by human reason (CCC 237).\n\nONE GOD: Catholics are monotheists. They inherit the Jewish Shema: \"Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one\" (Deuteronomy 6:4), and Jesus repeats it (Mark 12:29). The Trinity is not three gods (tritheism). \"We do not confess three Gods, but one God in three persons\" (CCC 253). Each person is fully God, not one-third of God.\n\nTHREE PERSONS: The persons are really distinct from one another because of how they relate (CCC 254–255). The Father is unbegotten. The Son is eternally begotten of the Father. The Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son. God is not one person wearing three masks (that error is called modalism). God is also not a Father who later created a lesser Son (Arianism). Because God is a communion of persons, \"God is love\" (1 John 4:8): love has always existed within God.\n\nSCRIPTURAL ROOTS: The word \"Trinity\" is not in the Bible, but the belief grows from it.\n• Jesus's baptism (Matthew 3:16–17): the Son is baptised, the Spirit descends like a dove, and the Father's voice speaks.\n• The Great Commission: baptise \"in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\" (Matthew 28:19). Note \"name\" is singular.\n• St Paul's blessing: \"The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Spirit be with all of you\" (2 Corinthians 13:13/14).\n• John 1:1: \"the Word was with God, and the Word was God\". John 14–16: Jesus promises to send the Advocate (Paraclete).\n• At creation the Spirit \"swept over the face of the waters\" (Genesis 1:2), and God says \"Let us make humankind in our image\" (Genesis 1:26). Christians read these as hints of the Trinity.\n\nTHE NICENE CREED AND ITS DEVELOPMENT: In the early 4th century Arius, a priest in Alexandria, taught that the Son was created and \"there was when he was not\". Emperor Constantine called the Council of Nicaea (AD 325). The bishops declared that the Son is \"begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father\" (Greek homoousios, \"of the same substance/being\"). The Council of Constantinople (AD 381) expanded the Creed to defend the full divinity of the Holy Spirit: \"the Lord, the giver of life… who with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified\". The Creed is professed at Sunday Mass. The Western Church later added that the Spirit proceeds from the Father \"and the Son\" (filioque). This is one point of disagreement with Orthodox Christians, who say the Spirit proceeds from the Father.\n\nROLES (but one God): The Father is creator and source. The Son became incarnate in Jesus and is redeemer. The Holy Spirit is the sanctifier who gives life, guides the Church and strengthens believers (for example at Pentecost, Acts 2, and in Confirmation). All three act together in every divine work (CCC 258).\n\nINFLUENCE TODAY: Catholics make the sign of the cross \"in the name of the Father…\". Baptisms are performed in the threefold name. They pray the \"Glory be\". Mass begins and ends with the Trinity. Believing in a God who is relationship encourages Catholics to value community, family and self-giving love.\n\nDIVERSITY: Orthodox, Anglican and most Protestant churches accept the Nicene Creed. Non-trinitarian groups such as Unitarians, Jehovah's Witnesses and The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints reject or reinterpret it. Jews and Muslims see the Trinity as compromising God's oneness (Islam's tawhid). Analogies such as St Patrick's shamrock or water as ice, liquid and steam help but are imperfect. The water analogy actually suggests modalism.\n\nKEY TERMS: Trinity, monotheism, person, consubstantial/homoousios, begotten, proceeds, Arianism, modalism, filioque, Paraclete, mystery."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","title":"Creation: the Genesis accounts, the nature of God, human dignity and stewardship","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, key beliefs: creation in Genesis 1 and 2; what the accounts show about the nature of God, the dignity of human beings (imago Dei) and humanity's relationship with creation (stewardship); different Christian interpretations of creation.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: Catholics believe God freely created everything that exists out of nothing (ex nihilo, CCC 296). Creation was an act of love by the whole Trinity. They believe Genesis teaches religious truth about God, humanity and the world, not a scientific timetable.\n\nGENESIS 1 (1:1–2:4a): God creates in six \"days\" by his word: \"Let there be light\". The order runs from light, sky, land and plants to the sun and moon, then sea creatures and birds, then land animals, and finally human beings. After each stage \"God saw that it was good\". At the end it was \"very good\" (1:31). Humans are made \"in the image of God… male and female he created them\" (1:26–27) and told to \"be fruitful and multiply… and have dominion\" (1:28). God rests on the seventh day, which is the root of the Sabbath.\nWhat it shows about God: God is omnipotent (creates by word alone), transcendent (beyond creation), orderly, good and the source of all life. The Spirit \"sweeping over the waters\" (1:2) and \"Let us make\" (1:26) are read as hints of the Trinity. John 1:1–3 adds that all things were made through the Word (the Son).\n\nGENESIS 2 (2:4b–25): A more personal account. God forms the man from \"the dust of the ground\" and breathes \"the breath of life\" into his nostrils (2:7). He places him in the Garden of Eden \"to till it and keep it\" (2:15). Adam names the animals. God forms the woman from the man's rib, because \"it is not good that the man should be alone\" (2:18). What it shows: God is immanent and personal, involved closely with his creation. Humans are both physical (dust) and spiritual (God's breath, the soul). Humans are made for relationship and marriage.\n\nHUMAN DIGNITY: Being made in God's image (imago Dei) means every human being has dignity, reason, free will and the ability to love and know God (CCC 356–357). \"The human person… is not just something, but someone.\" This underpins Catholic teaching on the sanctity of life, equality of men and women, and human rights.\n\nSTEWARDSHIP: \"Dominion\" does not mean exploitation. Humans are stewards, caretakers responsible to God for the world (CCC 373, 2415). Pope Francis's encyclical Laudato Si' (2015) says the Bible gives no basis for \"tyrannical anthropocentrism\". It calls for \"ecological conversion\" and care for \"our common home\". Catholics respond through recycling and energy projects in parishes, CAFOD climate campaigns and the annual Season of Creation (1 September – 4 October).\n\nDIFFERENT CHRISTIAN VIEWS:\n• Literalist or fundamentalist Christians (some evangelicals, \"Young Earth\" creationists) believe Genesis is a literal historical account: six 24-hour days, a world only thousands of years old.\n• The Catholic Church teaches that Genesis uses \"figurative language\" (CCC 390) to express real truths. It is \"true\" without being a science textbook. Pope Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) allowed study of evolution as long as the soul is understood to be directly created by God. Pope John Paul II (1996) said evolution is \"more than a hypothesis\".\n• Many Catholics and other Christians accept the Big Bang theory and theistic evolution: God works through natural processes. The Big Bang was first proposed by Fr Georges Lemaître, a Belgian Catholic priest and physicist (1927).\n• Some Christians support Intelligent Design, arguing that complexity in nature points to a designer.\n\nKEY TERMS: creation ex nihilo, omnipotent, transcendent, immanent, imago Dei, stewardship, dominion, literalism, theistic evolution, Laudato Si'."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","title":"Incarnation: Jesus as the incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, key beliefs: the Incarnation – Jesus as incarnate Son and divine Word, fully God and fully human; the scriptural basis for this belief (including John 1 and the infancy narratives).","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: The Incarnation (Latin in carne, \"in flesh\") is the belief that God the Son, the second person of the Trinity, became a human being in Jesus of Nazareth. He did this without ceasing to be God. Jesus is therefore one divine person with two natures: fully God and fully human (CCC 464–469). The Nicene Creed says he \"came down from heaven, and by the Holy Spirit was incarnate of the Virgin Mary, and became man\".\n\nTHE DIVINE WORD (John 1): John's Gospel begins: \"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God\" (John 1:1). \"Word\" is the Greek Logos, God's eternal self-expression and reason, through whom \"all things came into being\" (1:3). Then: \"the Word became flesh and lived among us… full of grace and truth\" (John 1:14). The Son existed before he was born as Jesus. The Incarnation is God personally entering human history.\n\nSCRIPTURAL BASIS FOR THE INCARNATION AND VIRGIN BIRTH:\n• Matthew 1:18–25: Mary is found to be with child \"from the Holy Spirit\". The angel tells Joseph to name him Jesus, \"for he will save his people from their sins\". Matthew links this to Isaiah 7:14: \"they shall name him Emmanuel… God is with us\".\n• Luke 1:26–38 (the Annunciation): Gabriel tells Mary she will conceive. \"The Holy Spirit will come upon you… the child to be born will be holy; he will be called Son of God\" (1:35). Mary replies, \"Here am I, the servant of the Lord\" (1:38).\n• Philippians 2:6–8: Christ \"emptied himself, taking the form of a slave, being born in human likeness\".\n• Colossians 1:15: Christ is \"the image of the invisible God\".\n\nFULLY HUMAN: Jesus was born, grew, ate, felt tired, wept at Lazarus's tomb (John 11:35), was tempted (Matthew 4), felt anguish in Gethsemane and truly died. He had a human mind and will. He was like us \"in every respect… yet without sin\" (Hebrews 4:15).\n\nFULLY DIVINE: Jesus forgave sins (Mark 2:5–12), worked miracles, accepted worship, called God \"Abba\", rose from the dead, and said \"The Father and I are one\" (John 10:30). Thomas calls him \"My Lord and my God!\" (John 20:28).\n\nCHURCH TEACHING: Early heresies denied one side or the other. Docetism said Jesus only seemed human. Arianism said he was not truly God. The Council of Nicaea (325) affirmed his divinity. The Council of Chalcedon (451) declared Christ is one person \"in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation\". Mary is honoured as Theotokos, \"Mother of God\" (Council of Ephesus, 431), because the one she bore is God (CCC 495). Christmas (25 December) and the Feast of the Annunciation (25 March) celebrate the Incarnation. At the Creed's words \"and by the Holy Spirit was incarnate…\" Catholics bow.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS:\n• Revelation: in Jesus, God is shown fully. \"Whoever has seen me has seen the Father\" (John 14:9).\n• Salvation: only one who is both God and human can reconcile humanity with God (CCC 457–460). \"The Word became flesh… to save us by reconciling us with God.\"\n• Example: Jesus is a model of holiness. The Incarnation also makes human life and the material world holy, which underpins the sacraments and the dignity of every person.\n• God with us: God understands human suffering from the inside.\n\nDIFFERENT VIEWS: Most Christians accept Chalcedon. Some liberal Christians see the virgin birth as symbolic and stress Jesus's humanity as a uniquely God-filled teacher. Jews regard Jesus as a teacher but not the Messiah or divine. Muslims honour Isa (Jesus) as a great prophet born of a virgin but reject that he is God or God's Son.\n\nKEY TERMS: Incarnation, Logos/Word, Emmanuel, Annunciation, virgin birth, two natures, hypostatic union, Theotokos, Chalcedon, docetism."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","title":"Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for salvation and grace","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, key beliefs: redemption – the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus for Catholic beliefs about salvation and grace.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: Redemption means \"buying back\" or rescuing. Catholics believe the whole Paschal Mystery of Jesus redeems humanity from sin and death and restores the relationship with God that was broken by sin. The Paschal Mystery is his life, passion, death, resurrection and ascension (CCC 571).\n\nWHY REDEMPTION IS NEEDED: Genesis 3 describes the Fall. Adam and Eve disobey God, and sin and death enter the world. Catholics call the wounded human condition that results \"original sin\" (CCC 396–409). Humans cannot repair this relationship by their own efforts. \"The wages of sin is death, but the free gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord\" (Romans 6:23).\n\nJESUS'S LIFE: His whole life is redemptive (CCC 517). Through his obedience to the Father, his teaching, healings and forgiveness of sinners, he shows God's love and restores what Adam's disobedience lost. Paul calls him the \"new Adam\" (1 Corinthians 15:22; Romans 5:19).\n\nJESUS'S DEATH: Jesus freely gave his life \"as a ransom for many\" (Mark 10:45). His death on the cross is a sacrifice of atonement. He took the punishment that sin deserves and reconciled humanity with God (\"at-one-ment\"). \"God so loved the world that he gave his only Son\" (John 3:16). Catholics believe this sacrifice is made present, not repeated, at every Mass (CCC 1366–1367). The crucifix, which shows Christ's body on the cross, is a reminder of this.\n\nRESURRECTION: On the third day Jesus rose bodily from the dead. The empty tomb is in Luke 24 and John 20, and the appearances to Mary Magdalene, the apostles and Thomas follow. The resurrection proves Jesus's victory over sin and death, confirms his divinity and is the guarantee of believers' own resurrection. \"If Christ has not been raised, your faith has been in vain\" (1 Corinthians 15:17). It is celebrated at the Easter Vigil, the high point of the Church's year, and every Sunday.\n\nASCENSION: Forty days after Easter, Jesus returned to the Father (Acts 1:6–11). He promised to send the Holy Spirit and to come again. The ascension shows that human nature has been taken into God's presence, so heaven is open to humanity (CCC 659–667). Jesus now intercedes for us, and the Church continues his mission.\n\nSALVATION AND GRACE: Salvation is being saved from sin and its consequences and brought into eternal life with God. Grace is \"favour, the free and undeserved help that God gives us\" (CCC 1996). Catholics believe salvation is God's gift, received through faith and expressed in love and good works (\"faith by itself, if it has no works, is dead\", James 2:17). Grace comes especially through the sacraments: baptism removes original sin, reconciliation restores grace after serious sin, and the Eucharist nourishes it. Catholics also believe in the Church's role as the \"universal sacrament of salvation\" (Lumen Gentium 48).\n\nDIFFERENT VIEWS: Many Protestants, following Luther, emphasise \"justification by faith alone\" (sola fide), though the 1999 Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification between the Catholic Church and Lutherans found broad agreement. Some liberal Christians see Jesus's death as a powerful example of self-giving love (moral influence) rather than a payment for sin. Some Christians understand the resurrection spiritually rather than physically.\n\nKEY TERMS: redemption, atonement, sacrifice, original sin, Paschal Mystery, resurrection, ascension, salvation, grace, justification."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","title":"Eschatology: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, key beliefs: eschatology – Catholic beliefs about life after death: resurrection, judgement (particular and last), heaven, hell and purgatory.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: Eschatology is the study of the \"last things\": death, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory. Catholics believe that death is not the end. Because Christ rose, \"life is changed, not ended\" (Preface for the Dead at the funeral Mass). The Nicene Creed ends: \"I look forward to the resurrection of the dead and the life of the world to come.\"\n\nRESURRECTION: Catholics believe in the resurrection of the body, not just the survival of the soul. At death, the immortal soul separates from the body and meets God at once. At the end of time, at Christ's Second Coming, God will reunite souls with their bodies, which will be glorified and transformed (CCC 988–1004). St Paul describes this: \"What is sown is perishable, what is raised is imperishable… It is sown a physical body, it is raised a spiritual body\" (1 Corinthians 15:42–44). Jesus's own risen body is the model. It was real (he ate fish, Luke 24:42–43), but no longer limited in the same way.\n\nJUDGEMENT: There are two stages.\n• Particular judgement: immediately after death, each soul is judged by Christ according to its faith and works and goes to heaven, purgatory or hell (CCC 1021–1022). The parable of the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16:19–31) and Jesus's words to the thief, \"today you will be with me in Paradise\" (Luke 23:43), support this.\n• Last (general) judgement: at the end of time Christ will return in glory and judge all people publicly (CCC 1038–1041). In the parable of the sheep and the goats (Matthew 25:31–46), people are judged on how they treated \"the least of these\": feeding the hungry, welcoming strangers and visiting the sick and imprisoned.\n\nHEAVEN: Heaven is eternal life in perfect union with God, Mary, the angels and saints, \"the ultimate end and fulfilment of the deepest human longings\" (CCC 1024). It is described with images (a banquet, the Father's house with \"many dwelling places\", John 14:2). Its essence is the beatific vision, seeing God \"face to face\" (1 Corinthians 13:12; CCC 1028). Most Catholics see it as a state of being rather than a physical place.\n\nPURGATORY: Purgatory is a state of final purification for those who die in God's grace but are not yet fully free from the effects of sin (CCC 1030–1032). It is not a second chance and not a lesser hell. Everyone in purgatory will reach heaven. The Church supports the belief from 2 Maccabees 12:46 (Judas Maccabeus makes atonement for the dead) and 1 Corinthians 3:15 (\"saved, but only as through fire\"). Catholics pray for the dead, have Masses said for them and keep All Souls' Day (2 November).\n\nHELL: Hell is eternal separation from God, freely chosen by those who die in unrepented mortal sin and refuse God's love (CCC 1033–1037). Jesus speaks of \"the eternal fire\" (Matthew 25:41) and \"Gehenna\". The Church teaches that God predestines no one to hell. Its chief pain is the loss of God, for whom humans were created. Pope John Paul II (1999) described hell as a state rather than a place.\n\nDIVERSITY: Most Protestants reject purgatory as unbiblical, since 2 Maccabees is not in the Protestant Old Testament and Christ's sacrifice is seen as fully sufficient. Some liberal Christians doubt a literal hell or believe in universalism (all will eventually be saved). Others believe in annihilation of the wicked. Some Christians hold that the soul alone survives, rather than a bodily resurrection.\n\nINFLUENCE: Belief in judgement encourages Catholics to live morally and care for the poor (Matthew 25). The hope of resurrection brings comfort in grief. Praying for the dead keeps a bond with them (the \"communion of saints\").\n\nKEY TERMS: eschatology, resurrection of the body, soul, particular judgement, last judgement, Parousia, heaven, beatific vision, purgatory, hell, communion of saints."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","title":"The meaning of sacrament and the sacramental nature of reality","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, the seven sacraments: the meaning of 'sacrament' and the sacramental nature of reality (God present in and through the material world); different Christian views on sacraments.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: A sacrament is \"an outward sign of inward grace\". The Catechism defines the sacraments as \"efficacious signs of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is dispensed to us\" (CCC 1131). Each part of that definition matters:\n• Efficacious: they actually do what they signify. Baptism does not just symbolise cleansing; it really cleanses from sin.\n• Signs: they use visible, physical things (matter) and words (form). Examples are water, oil, bread and wine, laying on of hands and the words of the priest.\n• Instituted by Christ: Catholics believe Jesus founded them (for example, \"Do this in remembrance of me\", Luke 22:19; \"baptising them\", Matthew 28:19).\n• Entrusted to the Church: they are celebrated by the Church through its ordained ministers.\n• Divine life: they give grace, a share in God's own life (2 Peter 1:4).\nSacraments work ex opere operato, \"by the very fact of the action being performed\" (CCC 1128). They depend on Christ, not on the personal holiness of the priest, though the fruit a person receives depends on their openness.\n\nTHE SACRAMENTAL NATURE OF REALITY (sacramentality): Catholics believe the whole of creation can reveal God. God is present and active in and through the material world. This follows from:\n• Creation: the world is God's good work (Genesis 1:31) and shows his \"eternal power and divine nature\" (Romans 1:20). \"The heavens are telling the glory of God\" (Psalm 19:1).\n• The Incarnation: God became flesh in Jesus, so matter itself can carry the divine. Christ is the \"primordial sacrament\", the visible sign of the invisible God (Colossians 1:15).\n• The Church: Vatican II calls the Church \"a sacrament or instrumental sign of intimate union with God and of the unity of the whole human race\" (Lumen Gentium 1).\nSo Catholics see everyday things, people, nature, art and relationships as potential windows onto God. Pope Francis writes in Laudato Si' that \"the entire material universe speaks of God's love\" (LS 84) and that the sacraments are a privileged way \"in which nature is taken up by God\" (LS 235). The poet Gerard Manley Hopkins, a Jesuit priest, wrote: \"The world is charged with the grandeur of God.\"\n\nSACRAMENTALS: Besides the seven sacraments, there are sacramentals: blessings, holy water, rosaries, crucifixes, medals and ashes. They prepare people to receive grace and sanctify ordinary life (CCC 1667–1673). They do not give grace in the same way the sacraments do.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS: Catholic worship is rich in physical signs, such as incense, candles, statues, vestments, holy water at the church door and kneeling. Catholics believe they meet God through the senses as well as the mind. It encourages respect for the environment and the human body, and the sense that ordinary life can be holy.\n\nDIFFERENT CHRISTIAN VIEWS:\n• Catholic and Orthodox Christians recognise seven sacraments (Orthodox call them \"mysteries\").\n• Most Protestant churches (Anglican, Methodist, Lutheran) recognise two \"dominical\" sacraments, Baptism and Holy Communion, because only these were clearly commanded by Jesus in the Gospels. Anglicans call the other five \"sacramental rites\".\n• Baptists and many evangelicals prefer the word \"ordinances\": acts of obedience and symbols rather than channels of grace.\n• The Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) and the Salvation Army do not celebrate outward sacraments. They believe all of life can be sacramental and grace is received inwardly and directly.\n\nKEY TERMS: sacrament, efficacious sign, grace, matter and form, ex opere operato, sacramentality, sacramental, Lumen Gentium, dominical sacraments, ordinance."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","title":"The seven sacraments, especially the Eucharist as 'source and summit' of Christian life","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, the seven sacraments: names, meaning and effects of Baptism, Confirmation, Reconciliation, Anointing of the Sick, Matrimony, Holy Orders and the Eucharist; the Eucharist as 'the source and summit of Christian life'; different Christian views on the meaning and importance of the Eucharist.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE SEVEN SACRAMENTS fall into three groups (CCC 1210–1212).\n\nSACRAMENTS OF INITIATION\n• Baptism (CCC 1213–1284): \"the gateway to life in the Spirit\". The signs are water poured three times with the words \"I baptise you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit\" (Matthew 28:19). Anointing with chrism, a white garment and a lighted candle follow. Effects: it removes original sin (and all sin), gives new birth as a child of God and makes the person a member of the Church. It leaves an indelible spiritual \"character\", so it is never repeated. Catholics usually baptise infants.\n• Confirmation (CCC 1285–1321): the bishop lays hands on the candidate and anoints the forehead with chrism: \"Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.\" Effects: it strengthens baptismal grace and gives the seven gifts of the Spirit (wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord, Isaiah 11:2). It makes the person a mature witness to Christ, like the apostles at Pentecost (Acts 2; 8:14–17).\n• Eucharist (CCC 1322–1419): see below.\n\nSACRAMENTS OF HEALING\n• Reconciliation (Penance/Confession) (CCC 1422–1498): contrition, confession of sins to a priest, penance and absolution: \"I absolve you from your sins…\". Effects: forgiveness of sins committed after baptism and reconciliation with God and the Church. It is based on John 20:22–23: \"If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven them.\"\n• Anointing of the Sick (CCC 1499–1532): the priest lays on hands and anoints the forehead and hands of the seriously ill, frail elderly or dying with the oil of the sick. Effects: strength, peace and courage, forgiveness of sins and sometimes physical healing. It unites suffering with Christ. It is based on James 5:14–15.\n\nSACRAMENTS AT THE SERVICE OF COMMUNION\n• Matrimony (CCC 1601–1666): a lifelong, exclusive covenant between a baptised man and woman. The couple themselves are the ministers, and they exchange vows and consent. Effects: grace to love faithfully and to be open to children. It is indissoluble: \"what God has joined together, let no one separate\" (Mark 10:9).\n• Holy Orders (CCC 1536–1600): bishop, priest (presbyter) and deacon. A bishop lays on hands and says the prayer of consecration. Effects: an indelible character to act \"in the person of Christ\", to celebrate the sacraments, preach and lead the people. Only baptised men are ordained in the Catholic Church.\n\nTHE EUCHARIST – \"SOURCE AND SUMMIT\"\nVatican II calls the Eucharist \"the source and summit of the Christian life\" (Lumen Gentium 11; CCC 1324). It is the source because all grace and the life of the Church flow from Christ present in it. It is the summit because all Christian life and worship lead towards it. The other sacraments are \"oriented towards it\".\n• Institution: at the Last Supper Jesus took bread and said \"This is my body\", and took the cup, \"This is my blood\" (Luke 22:19–20; 1 Corinthians 11:23–26). \"Do this in remembrance of me.\"\n• Real Presence and transubstantiation: at the consecration, the substance of bread and wine becomes the Body and Blood of Christ, while the appearances (accidents) remain (Council of Trent; CCC 1374–1376). \"My flesh is true food and my blood is true drink\" (John 6:55).\n• Sacrifice: the Mass makes present the one sacrifice of Calvary. It is not a new sacrifice (CCC 1366–1367).\n• Communion: receiving Christ unites Catholics with him and with one another. Catholics must attend Mass on Sundays and holy days, and receive Communion at least once a year at Easter. Holy Communion is reserved in the tabernacle and adored (Eucharistic adoration). Pope Francis's Desiderio Desideravi (2022) reflects on the beauty of the liturgy.\n\nDIFFERENT CHRISTIAN VIEWS OF THE EUCHARIST\n• Orthodox: real presence, described as a mystery without the term transubstantiation.\n• Lutheran: Christ is truly present \"in, with and under\" the bread and wine (sacramental union). Luther rejected transubstantiation.\n• Anglican: a range of views, from real presence (Anglo-Catholic) to spiritual presence. Holy Communion is central but celebrated less often in some parishes.\n• Many Baptists, Pentecostals and other evangelicals (following Zwingli): a memorial, a symbolic remembrance (the Lord's Supper/breaking of bread).\n• Quakers and the Salvation Army: no outward Eucharist.\n\nKEY TERMS: initiation, chrism, indelible character, absolution, contrition, consecration, transubstantiation, Real Presence, source and summit, tabernacle, memorialism."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","title":"Prayer: the raising of the mind and heart to God; formal and informal prayer; the Lord's Prayer","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, worship: prayer as 'the raising of the mind and heart to God'; the purpose and importance of formal and informal prayer and different views about their relative importance; the meaning and significance of the Lord's Prayer.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: The Catechism, quoting St John Damascene, defines prayer as \"the raising of one's mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God\" (CCC 2559). Prayer is a living relationship with God. For Catholics it is always addressed to the Father, through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. St Paul urges Christians to \"pray without ceasing\" (1 Thessalonians 5:17).\n\nFORMS OF PRAYER (CCC 2626–2643): blessing and adoration, petition (asking for oneself), intercession (asking for others), thanksgiving and praise. A common memory aid is ACTS: Adoration, Contrition, Thanksgiving, Supplication.\n\nEXPRESSIONS OF PRAYER (CCC 2700–2724): vocal prayer (spoken words), meditation (reflecting on scripture or the mysteries of faith, e.g. lectio divina), and contemplative prayer (silent, loving attention to God, \"a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus\").\n\nFORMAL (SET) PRAYER: Prayers with fixed words, often said together.\n• The Mass and the Liturgy of the Hours (Divine Office), prayed daily by priests, religious and many lay people.\n• Traditional prayers: the Our Father, Hail Mary, Glory Be, Apostles' Creed, Angelus (traditionally at 6 a.m., noon and 6 p.m.), grace before meals and the Rosary.\nAdvantages: they unite Catholics worldwide in the same words, are tested by tradition, are easy to remember in times of stress and express the Church's faith accurately (\"lex orandi, lex credendi\", the law of prayer is the law of belief).\n\nINFORMAL (SPONTANEOUS) PRAYER: Prayer in one's own words, in any place, at any time. It is personal and honest, like talking to a friend. Jesus prayed alone on mountains (Luke 6:12) and taught \"go into your room and shut the door and pray to your Father who is in secret\" (Matthew 6:6). Charismatic and evangelical Christians often prefer spontaneous prayer.\nDebate: Which is more important? Formal prayer can become mechanical \"empty phrases\" (Matthew 6:7), but it can also carry people when they lack words. Informal prayer is heartfelt but may be self-centred or inaccurate. Catholic teaching values both. The Mass is the highest prayer of the Church, and personal prayer prepares for it and continues it.\n\nTHE LORD'S PRAYER: Jesus taught it when the disciples asked, \"Lord, teach us to pray\" (Luke 11:1–4; Matthew 6:9–13). Tertullian called it \"the summary of the whole gospel\" (CCC 2761). It is prayed at every Mass, at baptisms and in the Rosary.\nStructure: an address (\"Our Father who art in heaven\") and seven petitions.\n• Three about God: \"hallowed be thy name\" (reverence), \"thy kingdom come\" (God's reign of justice and peace), \"thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven\" (obedience).\n• Four about our needs: \"give us this day our daily bread\" (physical and spiritual needs, including the Eucharist), \"forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us\" (forgiveness depends on forgiving others), \"lead us not into temptation\" and \"deliver us from evil\".\nMeaning: \"Our\" shows community, since Christians pray as a family. \"Father\" (Abba) shows a close, trusting relationship. It is a model: praise God first, then ask for needs. It commits the one praying to live the values they pray for (forgiving others, working for the kingdom). The doxology \"For the kingdom, the power and the glory…\" is used by Protestants straight after the prayer, and by Catholics at Mass after a short prayer by the priest.\n\nCONTEMPORARY: Apps such as Hallow and Pray As You Go (from the Jesuits in Britain), livestreamed Masses and online Rosary groups. Prayer groups in parishes; Taizé-style prayer.\n\nKEY TERMS: prayer, adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, formal/set prayer, informal/spontaneous prayer, meditation, contemplation, Liturgy of the Hours, Lord's Prayer, doxology."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","title":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, worship: the role and importance of popular piety, including the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: Popular piety means forms of devotion that grew up among ordinary Catholics alongside the official liturgy (the Mass and sacraments). Examples include the Rosary, the Stations of the Cross, processions, pilgrimages, novenas, the veneration of relics and the use of medals (CCC 1674). The Church teaches that these devotions \"extend the liturgical life of the Church, but do not replace it\" (CCC 1675). They should lead people towards the liturgy. The Vatican's Directory on Popular Piety and the Liturgy (2001) gives guidance on this.\n\nTHE ROSARY\nWhat it is: a meditative prayer using a string of beads. The person meditates on events (\"mysteries\") in the lives of Jesus and Mary while praying vocal prayers.\nStructure: it begins with the sign of the cross, the Apostles' Creed, an Our Father, three Hail Marys and a Glory Be. Then come five decades. Each decade is one Our Father, ten Hail Marys and a Glory Be, often followed by the Fatima prayer (\"O my Jesus, forgive us our sins…\"). It ends with the Hail Holy Queen (Salve Regina).\nThe mysteries:\n• Joyful: Annunciation, Visitation, Nativity, Presentation, Finding in the Temple.\n• Luminous (added by Pope John Paul II in Rosarium Virginis Mariae, 2002): Baptism of Jesus, Wedding at Cana, Proclamation of the Kingdom, Transfiguration, Institution of the Eucharist.\n• Sorrowful: Agony in the Garden, Scourging, Crowning with Thorns, Carrying of the Cross, Crucifixion.\n• Glorious: Resurrection, Ascension, Descent of the Holy Spirit, Assumption of Mary, Coronation of Mary.\nThe Hail Mary draws on scripture: Gabriel's greeting \"Greetings, favoured one! The Lord is with you\" (Luke 1:28) and Elizabeth's \"Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb\" (Luke 1:42).\nImportance: John Paul II called the Rosary a \"compendium of the Gospel\". With Mary, Catholics \"contemplate the face of Christ\". It can be prayed alone or in groups, at home, at wakes or vigils and online. It brings peace through rhythmic repetition, and October is the month of the Rosary. Mary is honoured, not worshipped. Catholics ask for her intercession: \"pray for us sinners\".\n\nTHE STATIONS OF THE CROSS (Way of the Cross)\nWhat it is: a devotion following Jesus's journey from condemnation to burial, usually through 14 \"stations\" (pictures or carvings around the walls of a church). At each one the person stops to pray and reflect. It began with pilgrims walking the Via Dolorosa in Jerusalem and was spread in Europe especially by the Franciscans, so people who could not travel could make a \"spiritual pilgrimage\".\nTraditional stations: 1 Jesus is condemned; 2 takes up his cross; 3 falls the first time; 4 meets his mother; 5 Simon of Cyrene helps (Mark 15:21); 6 Veronica wipes his face; 7 falls the second time; 8 meets the women of Jerusalem (Luke 23:28); 9 falls the third time; 10 is stripped; 11 is nailed to the cross; 12 dies; 13 is taken down; 14 is laid in the tomb. Some add a 15th, the resurrection. Some stations (falls, Veronica) come from tradition rather than the Gospels. In 1991 Pope John Paul II introduced a \"Scriptural Way of the Cross\" using only Gospel scenes.\nWhen: especially on Fridays in Lent and on Good Friday. The Pope leads the Stations at the Colosseum in Rome on Good Friday.\nImportance: it helps Catholics remember the cost of their redemption, feel sorrow for sin and unite their own suffering with Christ's (\"take up their cross daily\", Luke 9:23). It inspires compassion for those who suffer today, and modern versions link the stations to refugees or victims of injustice.\n\nDIFFERENT VIEWS: Many Protestants reject the Rosary because of its Marian focus and worry that repetition is the \"empty phrases\" Jesus criticised (Matthew 6:7). Some Anglicans use an Anglican rosary or pray the Stations. Catholics reply that the repetition is meditative and that the prayer is Christ-centred.\n\nKEY TERMS: popular piety, devotion, Rosary, decade, mysteries, intercession, Stations of the Cross, Via Dolorosa, Lent, Good Friday."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-catholic-christianity-paper2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","title":"Pilgrimage: the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage and different Christian views","specFocus":"AQA 8062 Catholic Christianity, worship: the role and importance of pilgrimage, including the study of one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage (Lourdes is used here; Walsingham, Rome and the Holy Land are noted), and different Christian views about the importance of pilgrimage.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY IDEA: A pilgrimage is a journey to a holy place for a religious purpose. Catholics believe \"pilgrimages evoke our earthly journey toward heaven and are traditionally very special occasions for renewal in prayer\" (CCC 2691). The Church itself is described as a \"pilgrim people\" on its way to God (Lumen Gentium 48; Hebrews 13:14, \"here we have no lasting city\"). Pilgrimage is a form of popular piety (CCC 1674).\n\nPURPOSES: to grow closer to God and deepen faith; to pray in a place where God's presence is felt to be strong (\"thin places\"); to seek healing of body or spirit; to ask for forgiveness or do penance; to give thanks; to honour Mary or a saint; to experience community with other believers; and to take time out from daily life to reflect. The journey matters as well as the destination, because it can involve sacrifice and simplicity. Jesus himself went on pilgrimage to Jerusalem for Passover (Luke 2:41–42), and the Psalms include \"Songs of Ascents\" sung by pilgrims (\"I was glad when they said to me, 'Let us go to the house of the LORD!'\", Psalm 122:1).\n\nSTUDY OF ONE PLACE: LOURDES (France). AQA lets you choose any Catholic pilgrimage site, and Lourdes is the most commonly studied.\n• History: in 1858, at the Grotto of Massabielle, 14-year-old Bernadette Soubirous reported 18 apparitions of a \"Lady\" (from 11 February). The Lady asked for prayer and penance and for a chapel to be built, and told Bernadette to dig at a spot where a spring began to flow. On 25 March the Lady said, \"I am the Immaculate Conception\", confirming a doctrine Pope Pius IX had defined in 1854. The Church recognised the apparitions in 1862. Bernadette became a nun and was canonised in 1933.\n• What pilgrims do: pray at the Grotto, drink or bathe in the spring water (the baths), attend Mass (including in the vast underground basilica), join the nightly torchlight Marian procession singing the \"Lourdes Ave Maria\", and take part in the Eucharistic procession and blessing of the sick. Many go to the Sacrament of Reconciliation, light candles and buy holy water.\n• Healing: the Lourdes Medical Bureau investigates claimed cures. Thousands of cures have been reported, but the Church has officially recognised only about 70 as miracles. For most pilgrims the \"healing\" is spiritual: peace, acceptance and renewed faith.\n• Today: millions of people visit each year. Many dioceses in Britain run annual pilgrimages with sick and disabled pilgrims, supported by young volunteers (helpers) and organisations such as HCPT, which takes disabled and disadvantaged children. The volunteering and care for the sick are themselves an expression of faith.\n\nOTHER CATHOLIC SITES: Rome (tombs of St Peter and St Paul; Jubilee Years, such as the 2025 \"Pilgrims of Hope\" Jubilee with Holy Doors); the Holy Land (Jerusalem, Bethlehem, Via Dolorosa); Walsingham, \"England's Nazareth\" (1061, Lady Richeldis), with both Catholic and Anglican shrines; Fatima (Portugal, 1917); Knock (Ireland, 1879); Santiago de Compostela (Camino).\n\nDIFFERENT CHRISTIAN VIEWS:\n• Catholics and Orthodox: pilgrimage is a valuable act of devotion, encouraged but not compulsory.\n• Anglicans: many go to Walsingham, Iona or the Holy Land.\n• Many Protestants, following the Reformers, are suspicious of pilgrimage. They argue that God is equally present everywhere (\"true worshippers will worship the Father in spirit and truth\", John 4:21–24) and that pilgrimage can encourage superstition or the idea of earning grace. Some Protestants visit the Holy Land as educational trips rather than pilgrimages.\n• Quakers do not practise pilgrimage, and some Christians see life itself as the true pilgrimage, or think the money is better given to the poor.\n\nKEY TERMS: pilgrimage, shrine, apparition, Immaculate Conception, grotto, Marian procession, miracle, Jubilee, penance."}],"rubrics":{"Beliefs":{"1":"AO1. Two 1-mark items open each question. (a) Multiple choice: four options, one correct answer – give the letter and the answer. (b) Short answer ('Give one…' / 'Name one…'): only the first answer given is credited, so write one precise point.","12":"AO2. Evaluate a statement: arguments in support, arguments against (including different Christian or non-religious views where relevant) and a justified conclusion. Levels: L4 (10–12) well-argued, reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge of relevant evidence, references to religion applied to the issue; L3 (7–9) reasoned consideration of different points of view with logical chains of reasoning and clear reference to religion; L2 (4–6) reasoned consideration of one point of view, or recognition of different views each supported by reasons (max L2 if no reference to religion); L1 (1–3) a point of view with reasons stated; 0 nothing worthy of credit. Up to 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms.","4":"AO1. 'Explain two ways in which… influences Catholics today' (AO1.2) or 'Explain two different Christian beliefs/views about…' (AO1.3). For each of the two points: 1 mark for a simple, relevant and accurate explanation; 2 marks for a detailed explanation (developed with an example, evidence, reference to different views or detailed information). For influence questions the development must show clear influence on how Catholics live or act; for 'different' questions the two points must genuinely differ.","6":"AO1.1. 'Explain two Catholic beliefs/teachings about… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.' For each of two beliefs: 1 mark simple explanation, 2 marks detailed explanation (4 max). Source of authority: 2 marks for naming AND applying a relevant source (e.g. a Bible passage, the Catechism, a Church document, the Creed) to the question; 1 mark for naming one without applying it, or for applying one without naming it."},"Practices":{"1":"AO1. Two 1-mark items open each question. (a) Multiple choice: four options, one correct answer – give the letter and the answer. (b) Short answer ('Give one…' / 'Name one…'): only the first answer given is credited, so write one precise point.","12":"AO2. Evaluate a statement: arguments in support, arguments against (including different Christian or non-religious views where relevant) and a justified conclusion. Levels: L4 (10–12) well-argued, reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge of relevant evidence, references to religion applied to the issue; L3 (7–9) reasoned consideration of different points of view with logical chains of reasoning and clear reference to religion; L2 (4–6) reasoned consideration of one point of view, or recognition of different views each supported by reasons (max L2 if no reference to religion); L1 (1–3) a point of view with reasons stated; 0 nothing worthy of credit. Up to 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms.","4":"AO1. 'Explain two ways in which… influences Catholics today' (AO1.2) or 'Explain two different Christian beliefs/views about…' (AO1.3). For each of the two points: 1 mark for a simple, relevant and accurate explanation; 2 marks for a detailed explanation (developed with an example, evidence, reference to different views or detailed information). For influence questions the development must show clear influence on how Catholics live or act; for 'different' questions the two points must genuinely differ.","6":"AO1.1. 'Explain two ways/reasons/teachings… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer.' For each of two points: 1 mark simple explanation, 2 marks detailed explanation (4 max). Source of authority: 2 marks for naming AND applying a relevant source (e.g. a Bible passage, the Catechism, a Church document) to the question; 1 mark for naming one without applying it, or for applying one without naming it."}},"markingSchema":{"dsl":"SCHEME AQA Religious Studies adaptable guidance\nCOMMAND ALL\nMARKS AUTO\nREQUIRE use accurate subject knowledge and answer the command word\nREQUIRE support religious or non-religious views where the question asks for them\nNEVER invent quotations, sources or official level descriptors\nFEEDBACK be specific, supportive and honest about missing content\n\nCOMMAND Explain\nMARKS AUTO\nAWARD 1 one accurate relevant point\nAWARD 1 a clear linked explanation\n\nCOMMAND Evaluate\nMARKS AUTO\nBAND 9-12 well-argued evaluation with a justified conclusion\nBAND 5-8 relevant discussion with some evaluation\nBAND 1-4 limited points with little evaluation","id":"aqa-rs-generic","references":[{"title":"AQA Religious Studies 8062 specification","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification"},{"title":"AQA scheme of assessment","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification/scheme-of-assessment"}],"sourceNotes":"AQA GCSE RS A uses 1/1/4/6/12 question structures and a separate SPaG overlay. Check the current official specification and mark scheme before relying on a mark estimate.","status":"adaptable","title":"AQA Religious Studies · check and adapt","version":1},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":7,"paper2":3},"meta":{"modulesTotal":10,"paper1QuestionCount":70,"paper2QuestionCount":30,"provenance":"Original practice questions and revision notes written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification, section 3.1.3 Catholic Christianity (beliefs and teachings; practices), following the question structure AQA examines from June 2026 (1-mark multiple choice, 1-mark short answer, 4, 6 with source of authority, and 12-mark evaluation). Not past-paper material and not copied from AQA papers, mark schemes, textbooks or revision guides. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official mark schemes. Short Bible quotations and references to the Catechism of the Catholic Church and Church documents are cited for study purposes.","qualification":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062), Component 1 (Paper 1): The study of religions – Catholic Christianity (8062/12)","questionTotal":100,"title":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Catholic Christianity"}});
